c1950gid: {y:'1950', t:'Joe Edd Giddens at Alapaha, Berrien County',
  pl:'ED 10-3, Alapaha, Berrien County, Georgia \u00b7 sheet 14, dwelling 100, household 75',
  cite:'NARA \u00b7 enumerated 11 April 1950 by Marion T. Harper \u2014 the sheet that had been missing',
  url:'https://1950census.archives.gov/search/?county=Berrien&state=GA&ed=10-3',
  cols:['Name','Relation','Age','Occupation'],
  rows:[
    ['Gidden, Joe E.','Head','52','<b>Farmer \u00b7 Farm \u00b7 O \u2014 own business</b>, 50 hours','me'],
    ['Saddie','Wife','47','Keeping house'],
    ['L. R.','Son','18','<b>Farmer\u2019s helper \u00b7 Farm \u00b7 P \u2014 private employer</b>, 50 hours'],
    ['Mary R.','Daughter','16','Keeping house'],
    ['Harvey','Son','14','At school'],
    ['Fred','Son','12','\u2014'],
    ['Jo Ann','Daughter','9','\u2014'],
    ['Louiel','Daughter','5','\u2014']],
  note:'<b>The sheet this project had given up on.</b> Joe Edd\u2019s page said for months that the 1950 census would settle the barbershop question and that it could not be found. It could \u2014 the family had simply left Atkinson County. The surname is written <b>Gidden</b> without the final s, which is why every search for Giddens in Atkinson came back empty.<br><br><b>It answers three questions at once.</b> The occupation column says <i>farmer, farm, own business, fifty hours</i> \u2014 a third census, twenty years after the first, giving the same answer. The address says <b>Alapaha, Berrien County</b>, which is why he died in Berrien in 1974 and why his son Coy died there in 1973: <b>they lived there.</b> And the household finally puts <b>Jo Ann</b> on a federal record, where before she existed only in an obituary and in a grandson\u2019s memory.<br><br><b>One line is left unresolved, deliberately.</b> The youngest daughter, aged 5, is written <b>Louiel</b> \u2014 the same rendering the 1940 enumerator used for her sister <b>Lou Ella</b>, who was born in 1924 and was twenty-six by 1950. By age and by birth order this line belongs to <b>Lucille</b>, born 23 April 1945 and the youngest of the nine. The page is read at full resolution and the hand is not ambiguous: it says Louiel. Either the enumerator was given the wrong daughter\u2019s name, or Lucille was called something at home that he heard that way. It is recorded as written, and the conflict is left standing.'},

