const PEOPLE = {

jaxon:{n:'Jaxon Brady Luke', g:'u', b:'18 Aug 2000', bp:'Douglas, Coffee County, Georgia',
  ev:'The starting point. Everything below is traced backward from here.', src:[], cen:[]},

richard:{n:'Richard Luke', g:'u', liv:1,
  ev:'Living. Son of Elmer Parker Luke and Sally Rudine Hall; brother of Tommy Luke. Independently corroborated — <b>Richard and Tommy Luke both served as pallbearers</b> at the 2023 funeral of their aunt Brenda Carol Luke Jewell, a printed record placing them in this family.',
  src:[S.ob('https://obituaries.tiftongazette.com/obituary/brenda-jewell-1088589275')], cen:[]},

andrea:{n:'Andrea Denise McKinnon', g:'u', liv:1,
  ev:'Living. Daughter of Andrew McKinnon and Lucille Giddens. Married Richard Luke at Mora Baptist Church, Mora, Coffee County — the church where her uncle Monroe McKinnon Jr. served as a deacon for many years.', src:[], cen:[]},

elmer:{n:'Elmer Parker Luke', g:'u', liv:1,
  ev:'Living — confirmed alive as recently as July 2023, when he and Sally were named among the survivors in his sister Brenda’s obituary. He appears as <b>"Elmer," age 5</b> in the 1950 census household on the Lax–Willacoochee road, placing his birth around 1944–45.',
  src:[S.ob('https://obituaries.tiftongazette.com/obituary/brenda-jewell-1088589275')], cen:['c1950l']},

sally:{n:'Sally Rudine Hall', g:'u', liv:1,
  ev:'Living. <b>Her parents were a total blank before this research and are now identified: Jesse Lee Hall and Mattie Cribb Hall.</b> Three separate obituaries of her brothers each name those parents <i>and</i> list "Sally Luke and husband Elmer" among the surviving sisters. That convergence is what makes it solid rather than circumstantial.<br><br><b>A fourth has now been read.</b> Her brother <b>Jesse James “Buck” Hall</b> died 17 January 2022; his obituary names their parents as <b>the late Jesse &amp; Mattie Cribb Hall</b>, says he was <i>one of eleven children</i>, and lists among the survivors <b>“Sally &amp; Elmer Luke of Willacoochee”</b>. Four independent notices, printed years apart in different papers, putting the same woman in the same family.',
  src:[S.ob('https://www.relihanfuneralhome.com/obituaries/joe-bud-hall'),
       S.ob('https://www.currentobituary.com/obit/262396'),
       S.ob('https://obituaries.tiftongazette.com/obituary/mitchell-hall-739677462')], cen:['c1950h']},

andrew:{n:'Andrew McKinnon', g:'c', b:'12 Jan 1937', d:'19 Sep 2009',
  bur:'Mora Cemetery, Coffee County, Georgia',
  ev:'Your family had him as "approximately 1937–2009"; the headstone gives exact dates, and the 1950 census puts him at 13 — precisely right for a January 1937 birth.<br><br><b>On the Army service: still unproven, but the usual evidence against it is worthless here.</b> The VA has no record and his stone carries no emblem — yet the VA lists only graves where <i>it</i> furnished the marker, and his looks privately bought. More telling: <b>his brother Arlen’s obituary never mentions military service either, despite a VA marker reading "PFC US ARMY | KOREA."</b> This family’s obituaries simply omit it. Finding Andrew’s obituary probably will not answer the question; his service file will.<br><br><b>A piece of family testimony that does not yet fit anywhere.</b> Your mother says: <i>“Papa Andrew — his granny was Matilda McKinnon.”</i> She did not say which side or which generation. There <i>is</i> a Matilda Carver McKinnon in these records, 1906–1942, wife of Leon McKinnon — but if the compiled links around her hold she would be Andrew’s <b>aunt</b> rather than his grandmother, and she was thirty-one when he was born. The name and the county fit; the generation does not.<br><br>That mismatch is a reason to keep the testimony open, not to force it or to discard it. Families are usually right about <i>who</i> and often loose about <i>which generation</i> — “granny” does duty for a grandmother, a great-aunt, and the old woman who raised you. Either a different Matilda is meant, or the relationship is a step sideways from the one the word implies. Recorded, unresolved.<br><br><b>It is no longer unresolved, and the family were very nearly right.</b> <b>Matilda Carver McKinnon</b>, 1906–1942, was your great-grandmother <b>Mary Jane Carver’s sister</b> — her 1942 Georgia death certificate names their shared parents, Elias Carver and Laura, and she is buried in the same Douglas churchyard as them. She married <b>Leon McKinnon</b>. Mary Jane married <b>Monroe McKinnon</b>.<br><br><b>Two Carver sisters married two McKinnon men.</b> That is why a Matilda McKinnon sits so large in this family’s memory, and why the relationship was easy to misplace: she was Andrew’s <b>aunt twice over</b> — his mother’s sister, and married into his father’s name. She died on 12 June 1942, when Andrew was five. A small boy’s memory of an aunt who died while he was that age, carried down two more generations, arriving as <i>granny</i>: that is exactly how oral history wears.<br><br>The testimony was right about the woman, the surname and the family. It was one step out on the relationship, which is the most ordinary error in the whole of family memory.',
  src:[S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/90560546/andrew-mckinnon')], cen:['c1950m']},

lucille:{n:'Lucille Giddens', g:'u', liv:1, b:'23 Apr 1945',
  ev:'Living. <b>Your family’s information was right:</b> her parents were Joe Edd Giddens and Sallie Mae Mercer. Proven by her brother Ernest Giddens’s 2011 obituary, which names the parents and lists "Lucille Birch of Douglas" among survivors — <i>Birch</i> being the paper’s misspelling of Burch.<br><br><b>One correction to family memory:</b> Burch is a <i>later</i> married name, not an earlier one. She married Johnny Burch around 2010, after Andrew died in 2009. The order is Giddens → McKinnon → Burch.<br><br><b>She is on a census sheet, five years old, and the name beside her age is not quite hers.</b> The <b>1950 census</b> of Alapaha, Berrien County, taken 11 April 1950, lists Joe Edd’s household down to the youngest child: a daughter, aged 5, entered as <b>Louiel</b>. By age and by birth order that is her — she was born 23 April 1945 and was the last of the nine, and her brother’s list of the children written from memory ends in exactly that place. But <i>Louiel</i> is also how the 1940 enumerator wrote her much older sister <b>Lou Ella</b>, born in 1924, who was twenty-six by then and out of the house. The original page has been read at full resolution and the handwriting is not ambiguous — it says Louiel. So the line is almost certainly hers and the name on it is almost certainly wrong, and both halves of that are recorded here rather than one of them being quietly dropped.',
  src:[S.ob('https://obituaries.tiftongazette.com/obituary/ernest-giddens-739580082'),
       S.ob('https://douglasnow.com/johnny-burch-85/')], cen:[]},

jack:{n:'John Wesley "Jack" Luke', g:'c', b:'13 Oct 1896', d:'26 Oct 1964',
  bur:'Brushy Creek Primitive Baptist Church Cemetery, Ocilla, Irwin County',
  occ:['Farmer','pr',
    'In 1950 the enumerator found him on a farm on the Lax–Willacoochee road and wrote <b>60</b> in the hours column — sixty hours in the previous week — then left the occupation column itself blank on his row. The hours and the place are the record; the word <i>farmer</i> is the inference from them.'],
  ev:'<b>His parentage is now proven twice over.</b> He appears as "John," age 3, in the 1900 Irwin County household of Lucius and Narcessia Luke, and again as "John C W Luke," age 12, in 1910.<br><br><b>The burial place is a correction:</b> he lies at Brushy Creek in Ocilla, not at Lax or Willacoochee — a cemetery that was not previously on the family’s list.<br><br><b>He married twice.</b> The Irwin County index records "LUKE, JOHNNIE — McMILLAN, CLYDE — 09/08/1918," a first marriage that produced Richard Cleo Luke Sr., the family’s "Cleo." Nealie was his second wife.<br><br><b>The doubt about that marriage is now settled.</b> A 1920 census entry pairing a <i>Clyde Luke, 18</i> with a <b>Wm L Luke</b> had raised the possibility that Clyde McMillan married Jack’s brother rather than Jack — which would have made Cleo a nephew instead of a half-brother. Reading the Irwin County register straight through answers it: <b>"LUKE W. L. — HANLEY CLYDE — 06/23/1918"</b> sits in the same index, three months before <b>"LUKE JOHNNIE — MCMILLAN CLYDE — 09/08/1918."</b> <b>Two different women named Clyde, two different Lukes.</b> The 1920 household is W. L. Luke and Clyde Hanley, and it says nothing about Jack.<br><br>In 1950 the enumerator found him on a farm on the Lax–Willacoochee road with Nealie and twelve children at home, and recorded him working <b>sixty hours</b> in the previous week.',
  src:[S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/41253001/john-wesley-luke'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:M3JC-3CC'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:ML2H-JFH'),
       S.mar('http://www.southgeorgiagenealogy.com/marrfix1.htm')],
  cen:['c1900','c1910l','c1930','c1950l']},

nealie:{n:'Nealie Hinton', g:'c', b:'17 Oct 1906', bp:'Levy County, <b>Florida</b>',
  d:'20 Feb 1983', dp:'Tift County, Georgia',
  bur:'Brushy Creek Primitive Baptist Church Cemetery, Ocilla, Irwin County',
  occ:['Keeping house','c',
    'The 1950 enumerator marked her <b>“H”</b> — keeping house — and then <i>no, no, no</i> across the work questions, with twelve children in the household. It is the census’s way of recording a working life as no occupation at all.'],
  ev:'<b>The most useful single fact about her: she was a Floridian.</b> Born in Levy County, Florida — corroborated three times over, by her headstone and independently by the 1930 and 1950 censuses.<br><br>That explains a stubborn negative. A search of the complete Irwin County marriage index — roughly 7,000 entries across all years — turns up only three Hintons in the county’s entire history, none of them hers. <b>The Hintons were never a Georgia family.</b> Surname mapping bears this out: Hinton is <i>five times rarer</i> in these ten counties than in Georgia as a whole.<br><br>She is found as "Neal Hinton," aged 2, in her widowed mother’s Levy County household in 1910. She may have been christened <b>Maggie Nealie</b>.<br><br><b>Her mother is proved; her father is inferred. Those are not the same thing, and this page now says so.</b> The <b>1910 census of Levyville, Levy County</b> enters the household as <i>Abagil Hinton, head, widowed, 26</i>, with Otelia 9, James 6, <b>Neal 2</b> and Mack 1. That is a federal enumerator recording the mother–daughter relationship directly, and it is far better evidence than the cemetery-website family links that circulate for this couple.<br><br><b>Mackie Hinton is a different case.</b> No record read here names him as her father. What there is: Abby is entered as <i>widowed</i> in April 1910, so a husband had died before then, and the baby of the house is called <b>Mack</b>. A widow with an infant named for the man she has just buried is about as good as onomastic reasoning gets — but it is reasoning, not a document. What would settle it is Nealie’s own <b>1983 Georgia death certificate</b>, or a Levy County marriage return for Abby.',
  src:[S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/41253006/nealie-luke'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MVKD-BS6'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:3WL3-KMM')],
  cen:['c1910h','c1930','c1950l']},

jessehall:{n:'Jesse Lee Hall', g:'c', b:'27 Dec 1903', d:'30 Jan 1986', dp:'Coffee County, Georgia',
  bur:'Willacoochee City Cemetery, Atkinson County',
  occ:['Farmer, own account','c',
    '1950: <b>“Farmer / Farm / O”</b> — the <i>O</i> for own account, working his own place — 48 hours that week. Two of his children were entered beside him at 48-hour weeks of their own, marked <b>“NP”</b>: no pay.'],
  ev:'<b>Newly identified.</b> Named as father in three of his children’s obituaries. Heads the 1950 census household at North Ambrose and First Street, recorded as "Hall, Jessie C., Head, age 48" — note the census initial "C." against the obituaries’ "Lee."<br><br>His occupation column reads <b>"Farmer / Farm / O"</b> — the O meaning he worked his own place rather than a tenancy, which in 1950 Coffee County was not the majority position. Eleven children were in the house.<br><br><b>He is now found twenty years earlier, newly married, and in a place that matters.</b> The <b>1930 census</b> has <i>Jesse Hall, born about 1904, at <b>Axson, Atkinson County</b>, with his wife <b>Mattie</b></i>, aged twenty, and no children yet — so they had married within a year or so of the count.<br><br><b>And Axson in 1930 is where the two halves of this tree were already standing beside each other.</b> On the same census, in the same small settlement, <b>Joe Edd Giddens</b> was farming a rented place at dwelling 99 with Sallie and three small children. Jesse and Mattie are your great-grandparents on your <i>father’s</i> side; Joe Edd and Sallie are your great-grandparents on your <i>mother’s</i>. Two families who would not be connected for another sixty years, written down in the same place in the same spring, by the same enumerator walking the same road.<br><br><b>His parents are now proved — and the candidate family this page used to carry was the wrong one.</b> Two censuses hold him as a boy, both in <b>Clinch County</b>, which is exactly why every search of Coffee and Atkinson came up empty.<br><br>The <b>1910 census of Militia District 586, Clinch County</b>, sheet 4B, enters <b>Henry Hall, 38</b>, head; <b>Gurdy Hall, 26</b>, his wife; and four children — <i>Analaura 9, <b>Jesse 8</b>, Henry 4, Colonel, an infant</i>. The <b>1920 census of the same militia district</b>, at <b>Mud Creek, ED 59</b>, household 143, sheet 7B, has the same couple ten years on — <b>Henry Hall, 50</b>, head, and <b>Luridy Hall, 37</b> — with <b>Jesse entered against the relationship <i>Son</i></b> and six others round him: Anlora 16, Henry Jr 12, Colonel 9, Joe 7, Marvin 4, and Glenn at one year three months.<br><br><b>Both censuses carried a relationship column and both say <i>Son</i>.</b> <i>Gurdy</i> and <i>Luridy</i> are one woman badly heard twice.<br><br><b>And the geography closes it.</b> Atkinson County was cut out of Coffee and Clinch in <b>1917</b>. The move from Mud Creek to Axson, where he turns up married to Mattie in 1930, is barely a move at all — a good deal of it was a line drawn on a map around a family that had not gone anywhere.<br><br><b>The old lead is dead, and it is worth recording why.</b> This page used to carry a Coffee County family — <i>Sam</i> and <i>Yeeta</i> at Tanner in 1910, <i>Daniel</i> and <i>Leetta</i> in District 1170 in 1920 — with a <i>Jessie</i> born 1900 who looked like a fit on name, place and age. <b>That Jessie is a girl.</b> The 1910 sheet gives her sex as female and her relationship as <i>Daughter</i>. The candidate family goes with her, and the page it was written on said the 1920 household would settle it. It did.<br><br><b>His own dates are now documented, and they were the thing this page said to check next.</b> Two independent records agree on the day he died. <b>Georgia\u2019s state death index</b> enters him as <i>Jessie Hall</i>, died <b>30 January 1986 in Coffee County</b>, registered on 6 February. His <b>headstone at Willacoochee City Cemetery</b>, in Atkinson County, gives <b>27 December 1903 – 30 January 1986</b>. A state index and a stone cut by his family, arrived at separately, saying the same date.<br><br><b>The birth date is the headstone\u2019s.</b> The censuses never could agree on it — 1902 in 1910 and again in 1950, 1904 in 1930, 1906 in 1920, and an absurd 1896 in 1940 — and the state index rounds it to 1901. Against that spread, a specific day and month cut in stone by the people who buried him is the better record, and it is what this page now carries.<br><br><b>He is buried in Willacoochee</b>, which is where this line began: the Mud Creek household he was born into sat in the strip of Clinch County that became Atkinson in 1917, and he went back into that ground eighty-two years later, about fifteen miles from where he started.<br><br><b>And a published obituary now names him, his wife and the size of the family in a single line.</b> His son <b>Jesse James “Buck” Hall</b> died at Tifton on <b>17 January 2022</b>, aged 82. The notice printed at Ocilla two days later opens with his birth on <b>29 December 1939 in Atkinson County, Georgia, to the late Jesse &amp; Mattie Cribb Hall</b>, and then states plainly: <i>“He was one of eleven children.”</i><br><br><b>That is the number the 1950 census page shows and the family repeats, printed in a newspaper by the people who buried him.</b> The same notice lists <b>Sally &amp; Elmer Luke of Willacoochee</b> among the surviving sisters, and records the siblings already gone: <b>Myrtle Shook</b> and <b>Judy Luke</b>, and the brothers <b>Henry</b>, <b>Pete</b>, <b>Mitchell</b> and <b>Bud</b>.<br><br><b>And the obituary raises a question about a twelfth name.</b> Counted out, the notice lists exactly eleven: Buck himself, the surviving Sally, Shirley, Myrtice and Chester, and the six who went before him. But the <b>1950 census sheet carries a <i>Lorraine Hall, aged 9</i></b> — born about 1941 — who is not among them. Either she died between 1950 and 2022 and was left off a brother\u2019s notice, which families do not usually do, or <b>“Lorraine” is the enumerator\u2019s rendering of a first name one of the eleven stopped using</b>. Judy, Shirley and Myrtice are each unaccounted for by age on that sheet, and any of them could be her. <b>It is recorded as open rather than quietly reconciled.</b>',
  src:[S.ob('https://www.relihanfuneralhome.com/obituaries/joe-bud-hall'), S.fg('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QVK3-35TL')], cen:['c1940h','c1950h']},

henryhall:{n:'Henry B. Hall', g:'c', b:'about 1874', bp:'Georgia',
  ev:'<b>Sally\u2019s grandfather, and until now the wall at the top of this line.</b> He heads a farming household in <b>Militia District 586, Clinch County</b>, across two censuses ten years apart, and his son <b>Jesse Lee Hall</b> is entered against the relationship <i>Son</i> on both.<br><br><b>1910</b>, sheet 4B: <i>Henry Hall, 38</i>, head, with <b>Gurdy</b>, 26, and Analaura 9, Jesse 8, Henry 4 and the baby Colonel. <b>1920</b>, at <b>Mud Creek, ED 59</b>, household 143, sheet 7B: <i>Henry Hall, 50</i>, head, with <b>Luridy</b>, 37, and seven children — Anlora, Jesse, Henry Jr, Colonel, Joe, Marvin and Glenn.<br><br><b>His birth sits about 1870 to 1872</b>, on those two ages. Both sheets give his birthplace, and both his parents\u2019 birthplaces, as Georgia.<br><br><b>He has now been found in 1900, and it undoes an objection this page raised against itself.</b> The <b>1900 census of Militia District 586, Mud Creek, ED 14, Clinch County</b> — household 13, sheet 1B — enters <i>Henry Hall, 24, single, born <b>May 1876</b></i>, living as <b>Brother</b> to the head, <b>Tally H. Hall, 26</b>. Same militia district as 1910 and 1920; same locality, <b>Mud Creek</b>, as 1920. Single in the summer of 1900, with his eldest, Analaura, born about 1901. The life course fits exactly.<br><br><b>And it resolves the arithmetic.</b> This page previously rejected the 1880 household because a boy of three in 1880 would have been born about 1877, which did not square with ages of 38 in 1910 and 50 in 1920. But the 1900 sheet gives <b>May 1876</b> — which agrees with the 1880 boy and not with the later two. <b>Two early records agree on 1876–77; the two later ones are round adult ages, and adult ages on census pages drift.</b> The objection was aimed at the wrong records.<br><br><b>So the 1880 household is now a strong lead rather than a rejected one</b>: <i>Moore\u2019s Mill, District 1061</i>, with <b>Jane Hall</b> and <b>Joseph 20, Jessee 16, Rosa Lee 12, Preston 10</b> and <b>Rosetta 8</b>. The brother called <b>Jessee</b> sits well beside a man who named his own son Jesse.<br><br><b>It is still not drawn, and the reason has changed.</b> It is no longer the dates — it is that the 1880 household does not read cleanly. <b>Jane is entered at 35 with a twenty-year-old in the house</b>, which is impossible as written; either her age is mis-indexed, or the head is Joseph and she is his mother rather than Henry\u2019s. The index reads her as Henry\u2019s mother; the page itself has not been read. <b>Reading that one sheet would settle it</b>, and Henry\u2019s own death record, naming his parents outright, would settle it faster.<br><br><b>His birth was carried at about 1871 until his draft card turned up.</b> The records certainly his — 1910 and 1920, where his son Jesse is entered against the relationship <i>Son</i> — give 1872 and 1870, and the card he signed gives 1874. That third figure, being his own, now sets the date.<br><br><b>Tally H. Hall</b> deserves a note of his own: born April 1874, head of the house Henry was living in, with a wife and small daughters, one of them called <b>Jane</b>. If the Jane Hall of the 1880 sheet is the mother, that granddaughter carries her name.<br><br><b>He has a middle initial, and a draft card that proves it belongs to this family.</b> A <b>World War I registration</b> was taken in <b>Clinch County</b> for <b>Henry B. Hall</b>, giving his birth as <b>1874</b> — and the nearest relative entered on it is <b>Lundy Hall</b>. That is a fourth spelling of Gurdy and Luridy, and it is <b>the first record outside the census to name her at all</b>. The card is also the only one of his records he supplied himself rather than having an enumerator guess at him.<br><br><b>Two more Clinch County record sets carry him</b>, both so far only as index entries: the <b>Georgia tax digests</b> for Clinch, which run to 1900, and <b>three Clinch County voter registrations</b> under the name <i>Henry B. Hall</i>. Neither has been opened. Tax digests place a man on particular land in particular years, which is the kind of thing that turns a name into a farm.<br><br><b>And there is a grave.</b> <b>Henry B. Hall</b> lies in <b>Mud Creek Primitive Baptist Church Cemetery</b> at Lakeland, in Lanier County, under a stone reading <b>7 June 1867 – 25 January 1956</b>. <b>Mud Creek is his own place</b> — the settlement he is enumerated in twice — and the name on the stone is the name on the draft card. It is very probably him.<br><br><b>But the stone and the man disagree about when he was born, and this page will not pretend otherwise.</b> The dates fall into two camps. <b>1867</b> stands alone on the headstone. Against it: <b>1874</b> on the draft card he filled out himself, <b>May 1876</b> on the 1900 census, and a boy of <i>three</i> in 1880. In between sit the enumerators’ guesses of 1872 and 1870.<br><br><b>The consequence is not small.</b> If he was born in 1867 he was 33 in 1900, not 24 — and the single 24-year-old Henry Hall in Militia District 586 that this page leaned on may be another man entirely. If he was born about 1876, the headstone is wrong by nine years. <b>His birth is carried here as about 1874</b>, the figure he gave himself, with the whole spread on the record.<br><br><b>One document would end all of this.</b> He died on <b>25 January 1956 in Lanier County</b> — well inside the era of full death certificates, and that certificate names both parents outright. It is orderable from the Georgia Archives, and it would settle his birth year, his mother, and his father in one page.<br><br><b>Though a brother may have answered the question already.</b> The 1880 household Henry sits in holds a <b>Preston Hall</b> — and Preston died inside the window where Georgia death certificates are free and already digitised. <b>Preston Hall, born 10 January 1876 in Clinch County, died 10 November 1924 at Pearson, Atkinson County</b>, married to <b>Recy</b> — the <i>Rochel Wilson</i> he had married in Clinch County on <b>10 June 1906</b>. That certificate names his father.<br><br><b>It says <i>Jake Hall</i>. Not Jesse.</b> A compiled tree gives this family\u2019s father as <i>Jesse Hall, 1821–1898</i>, and a state death certificate filled in by the man\u2019s own people disagrees with it.<br><br><b>And Jake can be found.</b> No Jake Hall heads a household anywhere in Clinch County — but there is a <b>Jacob N. Hall, born 1851</b>, enumerated in <b>Militia District 586</b> in 1910 with a wife <b>Alice A.</b>, and carrying two Clinch County voter registrations. <b>Militia District 586 is Henry\u2019s own district</b> — the one he appears in in 1900, 1910 and 1920. <i>Jake</i> is the ordinary short form of <i>Jacob</i>, and he is the only Jacob Hall in that county.<br><br><b>Put together it reads like this.</b> Jacob “Jake” N. Hall, born about 1851, and a wife <b>Jane</b>, born about 1845, raising a family in Clinch County — Joseph, Jessee, Rosa Lee, <b>Preston</b>, Rosetta and <b>Henry B.</b> By 1910 Jane is gone and Jacob has married Alice; the compiled tree puts Jane\u2019s death at 1885, which would fit. Both men this tree cares about, Henry and Preston, sit in that one 1880 household, and the only father\u2019s name any record supplies for either of them is <b>Jake</b>.<br><br><b>Still not drawn, and the gaps deserve naming.</b> A Jacob born in 1851 cannot father a Joseph born in 1860, so either Joseph is Jane\u2019s by an earlier husband or his age is wrong. No record yet read puts Jacob and Jane under one roof. And reading <i>Jake</i> as <i>Jacob N.</i> is an identification of a nickname, however well it sits. <b>What this pass has done is replace a compiled tree\u2019s assertion with a state record that contradicts it</b>, and aim the search at a man who can actually be found.<br><br><b>And he was found standing next door.</b> The 1910 census of Militia District 586 puts <b>Henry Hall on sheet 4B</b> and <b>Jacob N. Hall on sheet 5A</b> of the same enumeration district — consecutive pages, which is to say the enumerator walked out of one house and into the other. <b>Jacob is 59, with a wife <b>Alice A.</b>, 58, and nobody else in the house.</b> An old couple living next to a young family whose head carries the same surname is not proof of anything, but it is exactly the arrangement you would expect.<br><br><b>The 1880 census was then read out in full, and it cuts both ways.</b> There are only <b>twenty-two Hall records in the whole of Clinch County in 1880</b>, in three households — Jane\u2019s at District 1061, Abiga and Catherine Hall\u2019s at Magnolia, and a scattering of singles. The county was that small.<br><br><b>Jane heads hers alone.</b> She is entered as the head, with six children and <b>no husband anywhere on the page</b>. So whoever the father of that family was, he was not under that roof on census day 1880 — and <b>Jacob N. Hall was alive then</b>, twenty-nine years old, since he is still living in 1910. A father who is alive and not at home is a separation, a work absence, or a man the enumerator simply missed; it is not the ordinary shape of a widow\u2019s household.<br><br><b>That is the honest state of it.</b> A state certificate names Preston\u2019s father as <i>Jake</i>. The only Jacob Hall in the county lived next door to Henry. And the 1880 household those two boys grew up in had no father in it. <b>Those three facts fit together, but not tidily</b>, and the tree records them without forcing them into a line.<br><br><b>A third brother has now been found, and he settles something the dates could not.</b> The man Henry was boarding with in 1900 — <b>Talley H. Hall</b>, head of the house — is not a landlord. The relationship column enters Henry against <i>Brother</i>, which is the census stating outright that the two men were brothers.<br><br><b>Talley Howard Hall, born 27 August 1874, died 10 July 1929</b>, and lies in <b>Springhead Methodist Church Cemetery at Willacoochee</b>. He married <b>Mary E. Tyson</b>, and the <b>1910 census finds him at Pearson</b> — then still Coffee County — with Bula E., Mamie, Mary, Matie, Maggie, Johnnie and Robbie around him. His son <b>Talley Howard Hall Jr.</b> was born at Willacoochee on 12 July 1914 and is buried beside him.<br><br><b>Three brothers, one road.</b> Henry to Axson and the Lax country, <b>Preston</b> dying at Pearson in 1924, <b>Talley</b> farming at Pearson by 1910 and buried at Willacoochee in 1929. <b>The move out of Clinch County was not one man leaving home — it was a family relocating together</b>, into exactly the few miles of Atkinson County where Jesse Lee Hall would marry, farm and be buried, and where Sally lives now.<br><br><b>And it argues against the headstone.</b> In 1900 Talley is 26 and heads the house; Henry is 24 and single under his roof. That is the ordinary arrangement of a younger brother lodging with an older one, and it fits a Henry born in <b>1876</b>. A Henry born in <b>1867</b> would be <b>seven years older than the brother he was living under</b>, still unmarried at thirty-three. Not impossible — but the 1876 reading is the one that makes an ordinary household, and the stone at Mud Creek is looking more like the outlier.',
  src:[], cen:[]},

johnmwilson:{n:'John M. Wilson, Jr.', g:'c', b:'Feb 1840', bp:'Georgia', d:'13 Sep 1912',
  dp:'Clinch County, Georgia',
  ev:'<b>Luridy\u2019s father, and the newest name at the top of Sally\u2019s line.</b> The <b>1900 census of Militia District 586, Mud Creek, ED 14, Clinch County</b>, household 101, sheet 6A, enters him as head: <i>John M. Wilson, Jr., 60, born February 1840 in Georgia</i>, <b>married thirty years</b> — so a wedding about <b>1870</b>.<br><br>In the house with him: his wife <b>Helan</b>, 50; and <b>Rachael 24</b>, <b>Lurrida 18</b>, <b>Lilla 15</b>, <b>Joseph 13</b> and <b>Franklin 8</b>. The relationship column names each of them, which is what makes this a drawn link rather than a guess.<br><br><b>The <i>Junior</i> is the interesting part.</b> A man calling himself <i>Jr.</i> at sixty had a father of the same name still remembered — a <b>John M. Wilson, Sr.</b> of Clinch County, born perhaps in the 1810s. <b>That reading was wrong, and it is worth showing why.</b> There <i>is</i> another John M. Wilson in Clinch County — he appears in the same 1900 census at <b>Du Pont</b>, with a wife Martha and children Charley and Iola, and the county\u2019s voter rolls carry both men, one registered in <b>1889</b> and one in <b>1894</b>, one docketed <i>Sr.</i> and one <i>Jr.</i><br><br><b>But the older man was born in February 1836 — four years before ours.</b> A father cannot be four years older than his son. <b>These two are not father and son at all.</b> In a south Georgia county with two men of one name, the clerk wrote <i>Sr.</i> against the elder and <i>Jr.</i> against the younger and thought no more about it. The suffix is a filing convenience, not a pedigree, and a tree that reads it as descent invents a generation that never existed.<br><br><b>What the records do say is that he went to the war — and the file has now been pulled and read.</b> <b>An earlier version of this page got the regiment wrong, and the way it went wrong is the same trap it had just warned about.</b> A FamilySearch index line reading <i>John M. Wilson, Co. H, 26th Georgia, Clinch</i>, with a <i>Hellen Wilson</i> attached, looked conclusive. The actual application behind it is the <b>other</b> John M. Wilson — the one at <b>Du Pont</b>, who gives his birth as <b>1836</b> and his wife\u2019s age as seventy. Two men, one name, one county, and the index does not distinguish them.<br><br><b>His own service is recorded instead on his widow\u2019s sworn affidavit</b>, and it reads <b>Company H, 29th Regiment, Georgia</b>. The <b>26th</b> and the <b>29th</b> sit in the papers of two different men whose names were identical; this tree carries the 29th, from the document about him, and flags that the point is not perfectly clean.<br><br><b>A neighbouring file makes the 29th look right.</b> <b>Thomas Newbern of Coffee County</b> enlisted in <b>Company G, 29th Georgia</b> at Savannah in June 1863 and was captured at Peachtree Creek. <b>The 29th Georgia drew its companies from this corner of the state</b> — Lowndes, Clinch, Coffee and the counties around them — which is exactly the regiment a Clinch County man would have gone into. The 26th is the number on the <i>other</i> John M. Wilson\u2019s papers.<br><br><b>A third file makes it three.</b> <b>Thomas Giddens of Clinch County</b> enlisted on <b>6 September 1861</b> in <b>Company A, 29th Regiment Georgia Volunteers</b>. His widow <b>Martha E. Giddens</b> swore it at Clinch on <b>16 July 1891</b>, and set down how he was lost in one sentence: <i>“Taken sick and died in Hospital at Savannah Ga on the 26th day of March 1862.”</i> Six months a soldier. They had married in April 1859, and she was still a widow thirty-two years later when the state paid her <b>a hundred dollars</b>. She signed with a mark, and asked that the money be sent to her at <b>Milltown</b> — the town that is now Lakeland, where Henry B. Hall lies buried.<br><br><b>Wilson in Company H, Newbern in Company G, Giddens in Company A — all of the 29th Georgia.</b> Three families of this tree, three companies of one regiment. That is what a locally raised regiment looks like from below, and it is now the strongest argument that the <b>29th</b> on the widow\u2019s affidavit is right and the <b>26th</b> on the index belongs to the other man.<br><br><b>He was on the indigent roll when he died</b>, drawing <b>sixty dollars a year</b> — the pension Georgia paid veterans who could prove they had nothing. He died in Clinch County on <b>13 September 1912</b>, and the tax books of the county showed him and his wife returning <b>no property at all</b>.<br><br><b>Georgia paid these pensions to veterans who could show need, and later to their widows</b>, so the application itself is a document about poverty as much as about service. <b>The file is held by the Georgia Archives and has not been read.</b> Applications of this kind usually carry a sworn statement of service, a residence, sometimes a physical description and the names of men who vouched for him — which is a good deal more of a man than a census line gives.<br><br><b>And he was not long in Clinch County.</b> The <b>1880 census holds exactly one Wilson household in the whole county</b> — Anthoney and Annie <i>Wilsin</i> at Homerville, with four small children, and not his family. His daughter Rachael was born in Georgia in 1876 and Lurrida in 1882, so the Wilsons were in the state but somewhere else. <b>They arrived in Clinch between 1880 and 1900</b>, and where they came from is the open question on this line now that the Junior has turned out to be a red herring.',
  src:[], cen:[]},

helanwilson:{n:'Helan Wilson', g:'c', b:'about 1850', bp:'South Carolina',
  ev:'<b>Luridy\u2019s mother, and the first South Carolinian on this side of the tree.</b> She is entered on the <b>1900 census at Mud Creek, Clinch County</b> as wife of John M. Wilson, Jr., aged 50, with her birthplace given as <b>South Carolina</b> — while her husband and every one of her children is Georgia-born.<br><br><b>That single column is a migration.</b> Somebody carried her from South Carolina into south Georgia before 1870, when she married, and the Halls and Hughses on other branches of this tree came the same way. Her maiden name is not on the sheet; the <b>Clinch County marriage books for about 1870</b> would have it, and they are in the filmed Georgia Archives series.<br><br><b>Her children carry the family forward twice over.</b> <b>Lurrida</b> married <b>Henry B. Hall</b>; <b>Rachael</b> married his brother <b>Preston Hall</b> in 1906. Two of her daughters married two Hall brothers.<br><br><b>Her own voice is on a document, and she could not write her name.</b> On <b>4 October 1913</b> she went before <b>J. T. Dame, Ordinary of Clinch County</b>, and swore a <b>widow\u2019s application</b> for her husband\u2019s Confederate pension. It records that she was married to <b>John M. Wilson</b> in Clinch County on <b>8 August 1867</b>, that she remained his wife until he died on <b>13 September 1912</b>, and that she had not remarried. At the foot of it she signed <b>Hellen × Wilson — her mark</b>.<br><br><b>Everything she had is itemised on that sheet.</b> Against her husband\u2019s property at his death: <i>none</i>. Against her own: <b>fifteen head of cattle, one hundred and fifty dollars</b>, and nothing on any other line. The Ordinary certified that the county tax books showed her returning no property for 1908, 1909 or 1910.<br><br><b>And the file explains why this branch is so hard to see before 1870.</b> The form required a marriage certificate. The clerk wrote across the bottom of the page: <i>“No record of Marriage. Records all destroyed by fire in <b>1868</b>.”</i> <b>Clinch County\u2019s courthouse records burned</b>, so a neighbour, <b>Harris Sirmans</b>, had to swear from memory that he knew the couple had married in August 1867 — which mattered, because the statute only pensioned widows married before 1 January 1870.<br><br><b>One thing on the affidavit contradicts the census.</b> She swore she had resided in Clinch County <i>all her life</i>, while the 1900 enumerator wrote her birthplace as <b>South Carolina</b>. Printed forms invite that kind of shorthand and so do tired enumerators; the page records both and settles neither.',
  src:[], cen:[]},

luridyhall:{n:'Luridy Wilson', g:'c', b:'about 1882', bp:'Georgia',
  ev:'<b>Sally\u2019s grandmother, and a woman whose name no enumerator managed to write down the same way twice.</b> She is <b>Gurdy</b> in 1910, aged 26, and <b>Luridy</b> in 1920, aged 37 — the same wife, in the same militia district of Clinch County, with the same children round her both times. The two spellings are what tie the two sheets together, in the same way Pheby Spivey\u2019s son Mathew ties hers.<br><br><b>She bore at least seven.</b> Analaura, <b>Jesse</b>, Henry Jr, Colonel, Joe, Marvin and Glenn are on the 1920 page; the first four are already on the 1910 one. Taking the 1920 age, she was about twenty when Analaura was born and about thirty-five at Glenn.<br><br><b>Her maiden name is not on either sheet</b> — the census never asked. A compiled tree gave it as <b>Wilson</b>, and this page carried that as <i>lead only</i> for exactly as long as it took to find the household — see below. <b>The tree was right, but a tree being right is not the same as a tree being evidence</b>, and the difference is the whole reason this line was worth chasing.<br><br><b>A fourth spelling has now turned up, and on a better kind of record.</b> Her husband\u2019s <b>World War I draft registration</b> in Clinch County names his nearest relative as <b>Lundy Hall</b>. <i>Gurdy</i> in 1910, <i>Luridy</i> in 1920, <i>Lundy</i> in 1918 — three clerks, three guesses at the same sound. <b>It is the first record outside the census to name her at all</b>, and it is the one her husband stood in front of and answered. A compiled tree gives her dates as <b>1871–1953</b> and her surname as Wilson; both remain <i>lead only</i>.<br><br><b>Her maiden name is no longer a lead. It is Wilson, and a census page says so.</b> The <b>1900 census of Militia District 586, Mud Creek, ED 14, Clinch County</b> — household 101, sheet 6A — enters <b>John M. Wilson, Jr.</b>, 60, head, born February 1840, married thirty years; his wife <b>Helan</b>, 50, born in <b>South Carolina</b>; and their children <b>Rachael 24</b>, <b><i>Lurrida 18</i></b>, <b>Lilla 15</b>, Joseph 13 and Franklin 8.<br><br><b>Lurrida is the fifth spelling and the one that settles it.</b> Eighteen in 1900 means born about <b>1882</b> — against 1884 in 1910 and 1883 in 1920, which is as close as census ages ever come. She is living at <b>Mud Creek</b>, the very place Henry Hall was living that same summer, in the same enumeration district. And the relationship column enters her as a daughter of that house.<br><br><b>Then the two families cross twice.</b> Her sister <b>Rachael</b>, single and twenty-four on this sheet, married <b>Preston Hall</b> — Henry\u2019s brother — in Clinch County on <b>10 June 1906</b>. Henry married Lurrida around the turn of the same century. <b>Two Hall brothers married two Wilson sisters</b>, which is the second time this tree has caught that pattern: two Carver sisters married two McKinnon men, three generations later and forty miles west.<br><br><b>Her mother was born in South Carolina</b>, which is a new state of origin on this branch and the thread to pull next.',
  src:[], cen:[]},

mattiecribb:{n:'Mattie Cribb', g:'c', b:'about 1910',
  occ:['Keeping house','c',
    'Marked <b>“H”</b> in 1950. Two of her children, aged 20 and 19, were recorded on the lines below hers working 48-hour weeks on the family place for <b>no pay</b> — which is the same arrangement she was under, entered in a different column.'],
  ev:'<b>Newly identified.</b> Named consistently as "Mattie Cribb Hall" in three of her children’s obituaries; appears in the 1950 census as "Mattie, wife, age 40," keeping house for a household of thirteen.<br><br><b>Cribb is a genuinely rare and locally rooted name</b> — only about 3,600 bearers in the entire United States, and nine times more concentrated in these counties than chance would give. English, from the Old English word for a manger. Its rarity makes it one of the most tractable lines left to research.<br><br><b>A candidate set of parents now exists, and it is a lead and nothing more.</b> A second research file proposes <b>David Benjamin Cribbs</b> and <b>Martha “Mollie” Crews</b>, resting on cemetery memorials and a compiled online profile whose underlying census, marriage and draft records were never opened. It carries its own internal problem: the marriage is given as <b>1896 in one place and 20 October 1898 in another</b>, and David’s own parents differ between accounts. That is the signature of a line copied between trees rather than read off a record.<br><br><b>That lead is now a record, and the census the other file pointed at is the one that did it.</b> It named a 1920 household indexed as <i>“Don Cribb”</i> without opening it. Opened, the <b>1920 census of Pearson, Atkinson County</b> holds <b>Mattie</b>, aged about nine, with <b>Martha</b> and her brothers and sisters Lexie, Annie B., Louis and Addie M.<br><br><b>And the household is found ten years earlier, under a head whose name is legible.</b> The <b>1910 census of McAlpin, Suwannee County, Florida</b> enters <b>David B. Cribbs</b>, born about 1875 in Georgia, with wife <b>Martha</b> and children <b>Mattie, Annie B. and Louis J.</b> Same wife, same three children, ten years apart. <i>“Don”</i> is the indexer reading <b>D. B.</b><br><br>So her parents go onto the chart: <b>David Benjamin Cribbs and Martha</b>. The compiled sources that proposed them were right — they simply had never opened the records that prove it. Her mother’s maiden name, given elsewhere as <b>Crews</b>, is still only a compiled claim and is not accepted here.<br><br><b>She is on the 1930 census too, at twenty and newly married.</b> <i>Mattie Hall, born about 1910, Axson, Atkinson County, wife of Jesse Hall</i> — no children yet. Ten years before this she was a girl of nine in her father’s house at Pearson; ten years after it she would have a houseful. And it puts her, in 1930, in the same settlement as Joe Edd Giddens and Sallie Mercer — the other side of this tree entirely.',
  src:[S.ob('https://www.currentobituary.com/obit/262396')], cen:['c1940h','c1950h']},

monroe:{n:'Monroe McKinnon Sr.', g:'c', b:'4 Oct 1900', d:'12 Jan 1989',
  bur:'Arna Primitive Baptist Church Cemetery, Willacoochee, Atkinson County',
  occ:['Farmer, own account','c',
    '1950: <b>“Farmer / Farm / O”</b>, fifty hours that week, working his own land in Coffee County.<br><br>The sheet is worth looking at for a second reason. Almost every other household the enumerator wrote down on that page — the Jacksons, the Grahams, the Millses — is Black, and almost every one of them gives the same answer in the occupation column: <b>turpentine</b>. Monroe’s farm sat in the middle of a turpentine neighbourhood, and the page records the two economies side by side.'],
  ev:'<b>Their marriage licence has been found, and it dates a couple this tree had only from a census.</b> Coffee County Marriage Book E, page 381: <i>“You are hereby authorised to join <b>Monroe McKinnon</b> and <b>Mary Jane Carver</b> in the holy state of Matrimony”</i> — licence issued <b>11 November 1922</b> by W. P. Ward, Ordinary. The certificate on the same page returns it: they were joined <b>12 November 1922</b> by <b>Reverend Frank Harrell</b>, and it was recorded on 27 December. Their first child Edward followed in 1924.<br><br>W. P. Ward is the same Ordinary who ten years earlier took Amanda Ricketson’s widow’s affidavit at Douglas. One clerk, two generations of this family.<br><br><b>His parentage is now proven.</b> The 1910 census at Pearson records him as "Monroe McKinnon, Son, age 9" in the household of Art (George Albert) McKinnon and Martha — with Martha’s own father, John Rickerson, living there and labelled <i>maternal grandfather</i> by the enumerator.<br><br>The online tree’s "approximately 1901" is corrected to <b>4 October 1900</b> from his headstone. Named "the late Monroe McKinnon, Sr." in five of his children’s obituaries.<br><br><b>Note the geography:</b> the family lived at Pearson and later Mora and buried at Arna. Pearson and Willacoochee are Atkinson County today, but Atkinson was carved out of Coffee County in 1917 — which is why this family reads as Coffee in some records and Atkinson in others.',
  src:[S.mar('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/countyfilm/id/34266'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/61237192/monroe-mckinnon'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MLKH-2Z4'),
       S.ob('https://www.ricketsonfuneralhome.com/obituaries/monroe-mckinnon/')],
  cen:['c1910m','c1950m']},

maryjane:{n:'Mary Jane Carver', g:'c', b:'24 Nov 1900', d:'14 May 1982',
  bur:'Arna Primitive Baptist Church Cemetery, Willacoochee, Atkinson County',
  occ:['Keeping house','c',
    'The 1950 enumerator wrote <b>“H”</b> against her name and <i>no, no, no</i> across the three work questions that follow it — the standard entry for a farm wife, and one that counts none of what she actually did as work.'],
  ev:'<b>She turns up in a newspaper at eighteen.</b> The <i>Douglas Enterprise</i> school column of <b>19 September 1919</b>: <i>“We are sorry that Miss Mary Jane Carver is out of school on account of illness.”</i> A week later, on the 26th: <i>“We are pleased to state that Miss Mary Jane Carver has fully recovered and is with us again.”</i> Three years before she married Monroe McKinnon, and the same column names Vickers, Williams, Floyd and Dorminy families around her.<br><br><b>Their marriage licence has been found, and it dates a couple this tree had only from a census.</b> Coffee County Marriage Book E, page 381: <i>“You are hereby authorised to join <b>Monroe McKinnon</b> and <b>Mary Jane Carver</b> in the holy state of Matrimony”</i> — licence issued <b>11 November 1922</b> by W. P. Ward, Ordinary. The certificate on the same page returns it: they were joined <b>12 November 1922</b> by <b>Reverend Frank Harrell</b>, and it was recorded on 27 December. Their first child Edward followed in 1924.<br><br>W. P. Ward is the same Ordinary who ten years earlier took Amanda Ricketson’s widow’s affidavit at Douglas. One clerk, two generations of this family.<br><br>The 1950 census records her as "Mary J., Wife, age 49" — matching a 24 November 1900 birth exactly for an April 1950 enumeration, which is unusually precise for this set of records. Her maiden name is beyond doubt: five separate funeral notices name her "Mary Jane Carver McKinnon." Married Monroe on 12 November 1922, though that date is so far only a memorial claim.<br><br><b>Her parents were the weakest link on this side of the tree. They are now the best-evidenced thing on it.</b> Until today Elias Carver and Laura sat here on the strength of a <b>Find a Grave memorial</b> \u2014 a contributor\u2019s family links \u2014 and a second research file proposed a rival couple, <i>Rev. James Monroe Carver and Susan Celester Flanders</i>, on compiled evidence of its own. This page said plainly that what was missing was a record stating the relationship outright.<br><br><b>The record exists, and it says Elias.</b> The <b>1920 census of Douglas, Coffee County</b> enters the household of <b>Elias Carver</b> and his wife Laura with four children: <b>Mary J.</b>, <b>Matilda</b>, <b>Levadar</b> and <b>Ben</b>. Every one of those names was already on this tree as a sibling of hers, gathered from other directions entirely \u2014 Levada was read off the 1950 census in Elias\u2019s own house, and Ben was enumerated a few doors away. The 1920 sheet puts all four under one roof with their parents.<br><br><b>And a state record confirms the couple independently.</b> Her sister <b>Matilda</b> died on 12 June 1942 in District 748, Coffee County, and her Georgia death certificate names her parents as <b>Elias Carver</b> and <b>Laura Newbern</b>. Matilda is buried at <b>Carver Baptist Church Cemetery in Douglas</b> \u2014 the same ground as Elias and Laura. That is a government record, not a family tree, and it does what no memorial can.<br><br><b>The rival household was a coincidence of names, and an uncanny one.</b> The 1910 census of Pearson holds a second Coffee County family \u2014 James M. Carver Jr and Susan C. \u2014 with a <i>Mary J.</i> born 1900 and a <i>Matilda M.</i> born 1902. Two unrelated Carver households in one county, each with a Mary Jane and a Matilda of nearly the same ages. That is precisely the trap this tree keeps meeting, and it is why the sister\u2019s death certificate matters: it names a father, and the name it gives is Elias.<br><br><b>What is still inference rather than record:</b> no document read here states that the Mary J. of the 1920 Elias household is the same woman who married Monroe McKinnon in 1922. It rests on her age, her county, and her sister marrying a McKinnon in the same few years. Her <b>1982 death record</b> would close that last inch.',
  src:[S.mar('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/countyfilm/id/34266'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/61237207/mary-jane-mckinnon')], cen:['c1950m']},

joegiddens:{n:'Joe Edd Giddens', g:'c', b:'22 Nov 1893', d:'7 Oct 1974',
  bur:'Sweetwater United Methodist Church Cemetery, Pearson, Atkinson County',
  occ:['Farmer, on his own account','c',
    'Three federal censuses, twenty years apart, give the same answer in three different enumerators’ hands. In <b>1930</b>, renting at <b>Axson</b>, aged 38 by the sheet: <b>Farmer — Farming</b>. In <b>1940</b>, in <b>Militia District 1130</b> outside Pearson, aged 42: <b>Farmer · Farm · OA</b>, sixty hours in the week before the count. <b>OA</b> is the code for <i>own account</i> — working for himself rather than for wages — and beside him his son C. S., eighteen, and daughter Louell, sixteen, are marked <b>NP</b>: unpaid family workers, the same sixty hours.<br><br><b>And now the third.</b> On <b>11 April 1950</b>, at <b>Alapaha in Berrien County</b>, aged 52 by the sheet, the enumerator Marion T. Harper wrote him down as <b>farmer · farm · O</b> — the 1950 code for a man working in <i>his own business</i> — <b>fifty hours</b>, on a place recorded as a farm of three acres or more. Beside him his son <b>L. R.</b>, eighteen, is entered as a <b>farmer’s helper</b> for a private employer. The boy drew wages; the father did not. Twenty years, three counties, three enumerators, and the same two words every time.<br><br><b>So what about the barbershop?</b> Your mother Andrea, his granddaughter, says he owned a barbershop in Willacoochee, and that is exactly the kind of thing a family remembers correctly. But it is <i>not</i> what any of the three censuses says, and those are the only records that ever asked him the question directly. <b>The honest position is that farming was his living in 1930, 1940 and 1950, and the barbershop is undocumented.</b><br><br>That is still not the same as saying it did not happen. The censuses catch a man three times in fifty years. He was 36 in 1930 and 56 in 1950, and he lived to be 80 — <b>twenty-four more years after the last census that asked</b>. A shop kept in the sixties, or a chair worked on Saturdays alongside the farm, would leave no trace on any of those sheets. What would settle it now: a <b>city directory</b>, or his <b>1974 death certificate</b>, which asks for usual occupation.<br><br><b>Willacoochee had the trade in it.</b> The <i>Douglas Enterprise</i> of 17 April 1909 inventories the town — twenty-five general stores, two banks, three hotels, about 1,500 people, and <b>two barber shops</b>. He was fifteen that year. The Atkinson County papers name the barbers of Pearson and Willacoochee through the 1910s and early 1920s — J. F. Roberson, Pasco Morris, “Red” West, the Cox shop at Pearson, and in October 1922 a <i>Mr. Little Carver</i> who “accepted a position in the barber shop in Willacoochee” — and <b>no Giddens appears among them</b>. Those papers stop around 1922.'],
  ev:'<b>He is now one of the better-documented men on this side of the tree.</b> Two censuses, a headstone, a state death index entry, and a son’s obituary all agree on the same man: born <b>22 November 1893</b>, married <b>Sallie Mae Mercer</b>, farming in Atkinson County, dead in <b>1974</b> and buried at Sweetwater.<br><br><b>One small conflict, recorded rather than smoothed.</b> His headstone gives the death as <b>7 October 1974</b>. Georgia’s own death index gives <b>5 October 1974</b>, and puts the death in <b>Berrien County</b> — the next county over — while the burial is at Pearson in Atkinson. <b>That gap is now closed, and not the way I guessed it.</b> I wrote on this page that he had probably died in a hospital at Nashville and been carried home to be buried. The <b>1950 census</b> shows the family living at <b>Alapaha, in Berrien County</b> — they had left Atkinson altogether. He died in the county he lived in. It was the <i>burial</i> that travelled, back to the church his people were already in.<br><br><b>Two children turned up who were not in the family’s own account.</b> Ernest Giddens’s 2011 obituary named seven siblings. The censuses name two more: <b>Coy S. Giddens</b>, born about 1922, on both the 1930 and 1940 sheets, and <b>Harvey Giddens</b>, born about 1936, on the 1940 sheet. Neither appears in the obituary or on the cemetery’s family list, which usually means they died before 2011 without being memorialised, or moved far enough away to drop out of the telling.<br><br><b>Where their marriage record is, and why it is not here.</b> The Giddens and Mercer families are <b>Atkinson County</b> people — Pearson, Axson, and Sweetwater church — and Atkinson County has <i>no</i> marriage books at all in the Georgia Archives’ filmed series. Both indexed Coffee County books covering the period were read through and neither carries a Giddens–Mercer marriage. They married in their own county, and that county’s books have never been filmed. They are held locally by the Atkinson County Probate Court at Pearson.<br><br><b>Eleven children, and the written record had lost two of them.</b> Ernest Giddens’s 2011 obituary named seven; the cemetery list names seven; the <b>1930 and 1940 censuses</b> add <b>Coy S.</b> and <b>Harvey</b>. The <b>1950 census</b>, found since, adds <b>Jo Ann</b> at nine years old and shows Harvey at fourteen, which fixes his birth in 1935–36 and matches the Harvey Giddens born 27 July 1935 who died in 2008. Both of the lost two are now documented away from the census as well — Coy S. Giddens, born 22 March 1922, died in <b>Berrien County on 2 October 1973</b>, on the Georgia death index and the Social Security death index both, a year and three days before his father died in the same county.<br><br><b>And the family itself never lost them.</b> A grandson of Joe Edd’s — a son of Ernest — set down his own list of the children from memory, and it runs <i>C.S., Lou Ella, Ernest, Mary Ruth, Harvey, L.R., Fred, Jo Ann, Lucille</i>: the same nine the censuses and the obituaries give between them, including the two that had dropped out of every written account. That is recollection rather than record, and it is not what puts those names on this tree — the census sheets do that. But when a memory and a document that never met agree on a list, it is worth saying so.<br><br><b>His own parents are still unknown, and the obvious searches have now been run and failed.</b> He does not appear as a child or a young man in any Giddens household in <b>Coffee County in 1900</b> (28 households), <b>Coffee in 1910</b> (57), or <b>Atkinson in 1920</b> (74) — a hundred and fifty-nine households across the three counties where this family lived, and not one Joe or Joseph among them. He surfaces for the first time in <b>1930, at Axson</b>, already 38 with a wife and three children.<br><br><b>Nor is there a draft card, and that is the strangest part.</b> Nearly every American man born between 1873 and 1900 registered for the First World War, and unlike Georgia’s Second World War cards — which were destroyed — the 1917–18 registrations survive complete. Every man in the Giddens, Gidden, Giddins, Giddings and Gideon families who registered with a birth in <b>1893</b> and a birthplace in <b>Georgia</b> has been listed: <b>twenty-seven cards</b>. Not one of them is his. The only November 1893 birth among them is J. Howell Giddens of Nashville — the man eliminated below.<br><br><b>Put the negatives together and they make a shape.</b> No 1900 census. No 1910. No 1920. No draft card. No Social Security record of his own in the public extract — he appears there once, as a father on his son Luther Robert’s application, never as himself. Then, in 1930, a man of thirty-eight standing in a rented house at Axson with a wife and three children, and from that moment onward he is documented continuously for forty-four years. Whatever the explanation is — a household the enumerators missed, a name written down so badly nobody has recognised it since, or a boy raised under somebody else’s surname — <b>the first thirty-eight years of his life have left no federal paper at all.</b><br><br>His son Luther Robert’s Social Security application names <b>Joe E Giddens</b> and <b>Sallie Mercer</b> as his parents, which confirms the generation below him and nothing above. <b>There was one lead, and it has now been run to ground.</b> A <i>Joseph H. Giddens</i>, born November 1891, appears in the 1900 census of District 1157, <b>Berrien County</b>, in the household of <b>William A. and Sarah E. Giddens</b> — and that family can now be identified from Georgia death certificates. Sarah E. is <b>Sarah Eliza Sutton</b>, born 1862, daughter of <b>Newt and Phoebia Sutton</b>, dead at Nashville in November 1939. Her son <b>Henry Newton Giddens</b>, born 1886, died at Berrien in October 1943 and his certificate names them both: <i>W. A. Giddens</i> and <i>Sarah Eliza Sutton</i>. So there was a real Giddens household in the next county with a son called Joseph, born in <b>November</b>, within two years of Joe Edd’s own November birth.<br><br><b>The test was run, and the lead is dead.</b> The way to settle it was to find Joseph H. Giddens somewhere after 1910, because a man living his own life in Berrien County cannot also be the man farming at Axson. He is found, and he is not Joe Edd. His full name was <b>Joseph Howell Giddens</b> — that is the <i>H</i> — and he went by the middle one. The <b>1910 census</b> puts <i>Howell Giddens</i>, seventeen, back in <b>Militia District 1157, Berrien</b>: the same district, the same parents, <i>William A.</i> and <i>Sarah</i>, with his younger brother Charley beside him. On <b>27 August 1911</b> he married <b>Abbie Bennett</b> in Berrien County. His <b>World War I draft card</b> gives his birth as <b>4 November 1893 at Nashville, Berrien County</b>. In <b>1930</b> he is still in District 1157 with Abbie and their daughters <b>Loubell</b> and <b>Thelma</b>. In <b>1940</b> he is still in District 1157 with Abbie — <i>the same census in which Joe Edd is enumerated in District 1130, Atkinson County, with Sallie and six children.</i> He died <b>3 August 1946</b> and lies in the Old City Cemetery at Nashville under the name <b>Joseph Howell Giddens</b>.<br><br>Two counties, two households, two wives, the same two census days. <b>They are different men.</b> I am the one who put that lead on this page, as the single testable candidate anybody had produced; the test came back negative, so it comes off. What it leaves behind is still worth having — the Berrien family is now identified down to the names on the death certificates — but it is not ours.<br><br><b>Nor is the other one.</b> A second <i>Joseph H. Giddens</i> lived in the same corner of the state: District 586, <b>Clinch County</b>, born December 1893, son of a widow named Fanny, with brothers Henry J., Kyler and Clyde. His own draft card gives <b>12 December 1895</b>; his full name was <b>Joseph Hardeman Giddens</b>, and he died at Lakeland on 4 July 1950. He too leaves a separate, continuous life — District 1300, <b>Lanier County</b>, in <b>1940</b> with his wife Olie, and still there in <b>1950</b>. South Georgia simply had more than one Joseph H. Giddens, and not one of them is Joe Edd.<br><br><b>And the 1900 census does not hold him at all.</b> Every Giddens, Gidden, Giddins, Giddings, Gedings and Gideon child recorded anywhere in Georgia in the <b>1900 census</b> with a birth in 1893 or 1894 was pulled and read — <b>fifty-six of them</b>. That schedule asked for the <i>month</i> as well as the year, so a boy born in November 1893 ought to be unmistakable. <b>There is not one.</b> The only November 1893 birth in the whole set is a girl, Kathleen Giddens, and she was in Nashville, <i>Tennessee</i>. Joe Edd was six years old and he was somewhere; that census either missed his household or wrote his name down in a form nobody has recognised since.<br><br><b>And the family has now supplied the thing that may explain all of it.</b> Your mother says Joe Edd <b>grew up in an orphanage and never knew his parents</b>. That is testimony, not a record, and it is kept here as testimony — but it is the first account anyone has offered that fits a man leaving no trace at all before the age of thirty-eight.<br><br><b>It sharpens the search rather than ending it, and in a direction worth being exact about.</b> An orphanage does not explain documentary silence; institutions were <i>enumerated</i>. A boy in a Georgia orphans’ home in 1900 or 1910 appears on the population schedule by name, as an inmate, like anyone else. So the testimony and the negatives together point straight at the caveat the family attached to it themselves: it does not establish that <b>Giddens was his birth surname</b>. A child raised in an institution, or placed out to a family, can very easily carry a name that was not his father’s.<br><br><b>The test that follows from the testimony has now been run, and it comes back negative.</b> If a boy was in an institution he was still enumerated, and the census records him with the parent columns <i>empty</i> — which makes an institutional child unusually easy to spot in an index. Every male Giddens, Gidden, Giddins, Giddings, Giddons and Gideon child recorded anywhere in Georgia in the <b>1910 census</b> with a birth between 1892 and 1896 was listed: <b>fifty of them</b>. <b>Every single one is entered in a family, with a parent named.</b> Not one is an inmate, a ward, a boarder or an unattached boy, and not one of them is Joe Edd. The only Joseph in the set is the Clinch County boy, son of the widow Fanny, eliminated above.<br><br><b>So the count now stands at four.</b> No 1900 census, no 1910 census, no 1920 census, no draft card — four independent record sets, four clean negatives, all searched under every spelling the name takes. A man who was somewhere for thirty-eight years and is in none of them was not being written down as <i>Giddens</i>. Combined with the orphanage account, that is no longer a guess; it is what the records are saying.<br><br>That changes the question. It is no longer <i>who were Joe Edd Giddens’s parents</i> but <b>which boy, under whatever name, became Joe Edd Giddens</b> — and the records that answer that are institutional admission and placing-out registers, not census indexes. The Georgia homes of that generation kept them: the Georgia Baptist Orphans’ Home, the Methodist home at Decatur, the Hephzibah home near Augusta, the Georgia Industrial Home at Macon, the Appleton Church Home. Several of those registers survive in denominational archives rather than online.<br><br><b>So his parents remain unknown, and the free census records are now genuinely exhausted.</b> <b>What would settle it is his own 1974 death certificate</b>, which asks for the father’s name and the mother’s maiden name. Georgia holds it — he died in Berrien County on 5 October 1974 — and it is outside every free online window. That one is a $25 order, and it is the single best twenty-five dollars left to spend on this tree. <b>The 1950 census has now told us where to send for it.</b> He died in <b>Berrien County</b> because he <i>lived</i> in Berrien County, which means the <b>Berrien County Probate Court at Nashville</b> holds a copy alongside the state office at Atlanta — usually the faster of the two.<br><br><b>Three ways forward, in order.</b><br><b>1. The death certificate.</b> Georgia’s form asks for the father’s name and the mother’s maiden name outright. The informant in October 1974 would have been Sallie or one of the children, and they knew the answer. This is the only document that would simply tell us.<br><b>2. The funeral home’s own record.</b> Undertakers kept a family sheet for every service, and it asks the same two questions. Whoever buried him at Sweetwater in 1974 may still hold the book, or have passed it to a successor firm. It costs a telephone call rather than a fee.<br><b>3. DNA — the one that does not depend on paper existing at all, and it comes in two forms that answer two different questions.</b><br><b>The Y chromosome answers whether Giddens was his name.</b> A man’s Y passes to his sons unchanged, and to their sons. Joe Edd’s went to Coy, Ernest, Harvey, Luther Robert and Fred — and on to their sons. It did not go to you: your line runs through Lucille, a daughter. So the test is not yours to take. It belongs to a <b>son of one of his sons</b> — Eugene Giddens, Ernest’s boy, who set down the list of children from memory, would carry it exactly. A single Y-DNA test on any such grandson returns matches sorted by surname. If they are Giddenses, he was born one and the record simply never caught him. If they are something else, that something else is his father’s name, and the whole search collapses to one family. No other instrument on this page can settle that question.<br><b>Autosomal DNA finds the family, and the person to test is Lucille, not you.</b> She carries half of him; you carry an eighth. Her matches through him arrive as first and second cousins, in the hundreds of shared centimorgans, where yours would be distant and faint. Every match of hers that shares no ancestor with the Mercers, Browns or Hugheses on one side, and none with the McKinnons or Carvers on the other, is his. Cluster those and the couple they descend from is his parents — whatever the surname was. She is eighty-one. This should be asked for kindly, and soon.<br><b>4. His own hand.</b> He drew Social Security, so an <b>SS-5 application</b> exists at the Social Security Administration and can be requested under FOIA for a small fee. It asks for his father’s name and his mother’s maiden name in his own words. A man born in 1893 applying in the fifties would also very often have filed a <b>Georgia delayed birth certificate</b>, sworn with affidavits, naming parents and birthplace. Either might say <i>unknown</i> — and even that would be the orphanage story confirmed in his own writing.<br><b>5. The institutions themselves — but only once there is a name or a place to aim at.</b> The Georgia Baptist home’s registers are at Mercer University, the Methodist home’s at Emory, Bethesda’s at the Georgia Historical Society. They are searched by admission year and age, not surname, which is why they can work where every census failed. But there are half a dozen of them, and the DNA or the SS-5 is what says which.<br><br><b>The order, then:</b> the Y-test and Lucille’s kit first, because they need no paper to exist; his SS-5 and death certificate next, because they are cheap and in his own hand; the registers last, aimed by what the first two return.<br><br><b>One unchecked lead, recorded so it is not lost.</b> The 1920 census of <b>Pearson, Atkinson County</b> — his own town — holds a <i>J. M. Giddens</i>, born about 1891, with a wife indexed <i>Darrel</i>. The initials are wrong and the wife is not Sallie, whom he did not marry until about 1921, so it is probably another man. But it is the right surname at the right age in the right town in the one census year that is otherwise blank for him, and nobody has looked at the page itself.<br><br><b>Giddens is one of the most locally rooted names in your tree</b> — about twelve times more concentrated in these counties than chance, and in Berrien County thirty-four times. English, habitational, from one of several villages: Giddinge in Kent, Gedding in Suffolk, or the Gidding villages near Huntingdon.',
  src:[S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/32978224/joe_edd-giddens'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:3MBR-92M'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:K728-FFZ'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:K72N-DMT')],
  cen:['c1930gid','c1940gid','c1950gid']},

sallymercer:{n:'Sallie Mae Mercer', g:'c', b:'6 Nov 1901', bp:'Clinch County, Georgia', d:'18 Sep 1992',
  bur:'Sweetwater United Methodist Church Cemetery, Pearson, Atkinson County',
  ev:'<b>Her parents are proved.</b> The 1910 federal census of <b>Militia District 1224, Clinch County</b> lists the household of Chas R. Mercer, 39, and his wife Ida, 36 \u2014 and seventh in the list of children, <i>Sally, daughter, aged 8.</i> That is a primary record placing her in her parents\u2019 house, and it closes a link that until now rested on a cemetery website.<br><br><b>It also dates the marriage.</b> Both parents are entered as "m 19" \u2014 married nineteen years, first marriage for both \u2014 which puts the wedding in <b>1891</b>, independently matching the date the cemetery gives.<br><br>Family tradition called her "Sally Mercer"; her full name was <b>Sallie Mae Mercer</b>. Her brother Ernest\u2019s 2011 obituary names her as "Sally Mercer Giddens." She outlived Joe Edd by eighteen years and lies beside him at Sweetwater.<br><br><b>The living memory agrees with the sheet.</b> A granddaughter of <b>Lucy Mercer Morgan</b> — the <i>Lucy, Daughter, 5</i> entered three lines below Sally in 1910 — says plainly that Lucy and Sallie were sisters. She was not reading a census when she said it. The 1910 page is what proves the relationship; this is simply the same fact arriving from the other direction, eighty years later, through people rather than paper.<br><br><b>One conflict stays on the record.</b> Her brother Denis\u2019s 1925 death certificate puts his birth at <b>12 February 1902</b>, three months after her own headstone date of 6 November 1901 \u2014 impossible for two children of one mother. The 1910 census gives her age as 8 in April, which fits a November 1901 birth and leaves the certificate as the odd one out. A grieving father guessed at a date, most likely.',
  src:[S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MLV1-45W'),
       S.ob('https://obituaries.tiftongazette.com/obituary/ernest-giddens-739580082'),
       S.ga('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/gadeaths/id/262038'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/85831793/sallie_mae-giddens')],
  cen:['c1910mercer']},

charliemercer:{n:'Charles Robert "Charlie" Mercer', g:'c', b:'March 1871', bp:'Bulloch County, Georgia',
  d:'3 Sep 1943', dp:'Pearson, Atkinson County', bur:'Sweetwater United Methodist Church Cemetery, Pearson',
  occ:['Farmer, general farm','c',
    'The 1910 census of Clinch County enters him as <b>Farmer</b>, industry <b>General farm</b> \u2014 working for himself, with his two eldest boys, 15 and 13, entered beneath him as <i>farm labour, home farm</i>. By the time he died in 1943 his certificate gives the usual occupation as a single word: <b>Retired</b>.'],
  ev:'<b>His own death certificate settles the generation above him.</b> Georgia certificate <b>18749</b>, filed at Pearson on 10 September 1943, names his father as <b>Jim Mercer</b> and his mother, in her maiden name, as <b>Sarah Robinson</b> \u2014 both birthplaces entered as "Unknown." The informant was <b>L. R. Mercer</b>: Luther Robert, his eldest son. A son burying his father is as close to the source as this line gets.<br><br><b>And it moves where the Mercers came from.</b> The cemetery says he was born in <b>Lowndes County</b> in <b>November 1869</b>. The certificate says <b>March 1871</b> in <b>Bulloch County</b> \u2014 a different county, a hundred and fifty miles northeast, and a different year. Two censuses back the certificate: 1900 gives his birth as December 1871, 1910 records him as 39. <b>Three records against one headstone.</b> This tree follows the records and keeps the stone on the page.<br><br>Bulloch County matters because it is where the Mercers actually cluster. Search the Georgia death registers for the surname and they pile up in <b>Candler, Bulloch, Emanuel and Effingham</b> \u2014 Candler was carved out of Bulloch in 1914 \u2014 not in Lowndes.<br><br><b>The marriage itself has now been found.</b> Clinch County Marriage Book 1889–1899, page 113: a licence from Ordinary <b>George Cornelius</b> authorising the joining of <b>Charles R. Mercer and Ida Brown</b>, returned with the minister’s certificate that they were joined on the <b>24th day of May</b> by <b>J. A. Osteen</b>. <b>The year is the one thing the page will not settle.</b> The licence is dated in figures <b>1892</b>; the return underneath it is written out as <b>"Eighteen Hundred and Ninety one."</b> A licence cannot post-date the wedding it authorises, so one of the two is a slip of the pen. The pages either side of it in the book are firmly 1892; the 1910 census, marking them both "m 19," points to 1891. Both readings are on this site because the document carries both. They were at <b>Magnolia, Clinch County</b> in 1900 and in <b>Militia District 1224</b> of the same county in 1910; when Atkinson County was carved out of Clinch and Coffee in 1917 the family did not move \u2014 the county line moved around them. His certificate records 38 years\u2019 residence, reaching back to about 1905.',
  src:[S.ga('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QLRP-M4YN'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MLV1-45W'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:M3N7-P97'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/32978358/charles_robert-mercer')],
  cen:['c1910mercer']},

idabrown:{n:'Ida Frances Brown', g:'c', b:'4 Apr 1874', bp:'Clinch County, Georgia',
  d:'25 Jan 1919', dp:'Atkinson County', bur:'Sweetwater United Methodist Church Cemetery, Pearson',
  ev:'<b>Three independent records agree on where she came from.</b> Her headstone gives her birth as 4 April 1874 in <b>Clinch County</b>; her son Denis\u2019s 1925 death certificate gives the mother\u2019s birthplace as <b>"Clinch Co Ga"</b>; and her sister Kizzie\u2019s 1935 certificate gives her own birthplace as <b>Clinch County</b> too. None of the three copied from the others.<br><br>She married <b>Charles R. Mercer</b> at Clinch County on the <b>24th of May</b> — the licence issued three days earlier by Ordinary George Cornelius, the couple joined by J. A. Osteen, the whole of it entered on page 113 of the county marriage book. The year on that page is written two ways and they do not agree; see the record itself on the document shelf. She bore at least eleven children. The 1910 census catches ten of them alive in one house, the youngest a month old.<br><br><b>She died on 25 January 1919, aged forty-four</b>, in the last weeks of the influenza pandemic. Her youngest, Doris, was one.<br><br><b>Her death was never registered by the State, and that is a finding rather than a gap.</b> Georgia began recording deaths in 1919, but the machinery took years to reach the pine country. For the whole of Atkinson County in the whole of 1919 the State holds <b>nine</b> death certificates, and none of them is hers. There is no document to order.<br><br><b>Her parents are named by a record \u2014 just not by one of hers.</b> Her sister Kizzie\u2019s 1935 certificate names <b>James Brown</b> and <b>Sallie Hughes</b>. That Ida and Kizzie were sisters still rests on the cemetery\u2019s family links, which is why her line up to them is drawn as probable rather than proved. <b>The arithmetic now comes very close indeed.</b> The 1870 census of District 1224 shows James and Sarah with three daughters — N. E., M. J. and Ruth, the last eleven months old. The 1900 census records Sarah as the mother of <b>eight</b> children, <b>eight</b> living, and names four more: Kizzie, Crecy, Charley and Mattie. That is <b>seven</b> children with names, and the only gap left for the eighth is <b>between January 1871 and August 1877</b>. Ida Frances Brown was born on <b>4 April 1874, in Clinch County</b>. She fits the hole exactly — by date, by county, and by being the one daughter old enough to be married and gone in 1900 without appearing anywhere else. <b>It is still not her name on a page</b>, which is why she is drawn as a probable daughter and not a proven one. The 1880 census of Clinch County would end it, and the 1880 index does not hold this family under any spelling tried.<br><br><b>She is now on a second census with her husband, and it settles the date this tree had left open.</b> The <b>1900 census of Magnolia, Clinch County</b> enumerates <b>Charles R. Mercer</b> and Ida together — her birth given as <b>April 1874</b>, matching her headstone — with four children: <b>George, Robert M., Maud and William D.</b> Robert M. and the others are new to this tree.<br><br>That sheet also records the marriage at <b>1891</b>. The Clinch County marriage book contradicted itself on the year — 1892 in figures at the top of the page, <i>“Eighteen Hundred and Ninety one”</i> in the minister’s return beneath it — and this tree recorded the conflict rather than choosing. <b>The 1900 census is the third record that was needed, and it says 1891</b>, as does the 1910 sheet. The words were right; the figures were the slip.<br><br><b>Her own death certificate does not exist, and there is a plain reason.</b> She died on <b>25 January 1919</b>. Georgia began statewide death registration <i>that month</i>, and rural compliance in the first year was close to nil. A search of the state’s 1914–27 death collection returns nothing for her. That certificate would have named her parents outright and closed the last gap on this line — it was never written. <b>Her maiden name is nevertheless attested three ways now:</b> the marriage book, the 1910 census, and her husband’s 1943 death certificate, which the state indexed under <i>Ida Brown Mercer</i>.',
  src:[S.ga('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QJXM-K6TN'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MLV1-45W'),
       S.ga('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/gadeaths/id/262038'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/32978361/ida_frances-mercer')],
  cen:['c1910mercer']},

jamesrmercer:{n:'"Jim" Mercer', g:'pr', b:'abt 1822', d:'1910',
  ev:'<b>He exists on a state record, under the name his son\u2019s family used for him.</b> Box 12 of Charlie Mercer\u2019s 1943 death certificate reads simply <b>Jim Mercer</b>, and box 13, his birthplace, reads <b>Unknown</b> \u2014 which tells you something in itself: by 1943 his own grandson could not say where he was born.<br><br>The Sweetwater cemetery records give him as <b>James R. Mercer, 1822\u20131910</b>, and those dates have no source attached to them. He died <b>nine years before Georgia began registering deaths</b>, so there is no certificate of his own to find. What there is: the 1870 and 1880 censuses of <b>Bulloch County</b>, now that his son\u2019s certificate points there rather than at Lowndes.',
  src:[S.ga('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QLRP-M4YN'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/32978358/charles_robert-mercer')], cen:[]},

bedona:{n:'Sarah Robinson', g:'pr', b:'abt 1833', d:'1889',
  ev:'<b>The surname is confirmed; the given name is disputed.</b> Charlie Mercer\u2019s 1943 death certificate gives his mother\u2019s maiden name as <b>Sarah Robinson</b>, birthplace unknown \u2014 reported by her own grandson. A handwritten note on the family\u2019s cemetery page calls her <b>Bedona Robinson, 1833\u20131889</b>, with no source of any kind.<br><br>The two are not necessarily in conflict. <i>Sarah Bedona</i> would reconcile them, and a woman known in life by her second name is among the commonest things in this whole tree. But only one of the two statements is on a state record, and it says Sarah. <b>Robinson is the part that is now real</b>, and it is a new surname to chase in Bulloch County.',
  src:[S.ga('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QLRP-M4YN'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/32978358/charles_robert-mercer')], cen:[]},

jamesbrown:{n:'James Brown', g:'c', b:'Sept 1828', bp:'Georgia', d:'1900',
  ev:'<b>He is in the 1900 census in his own right, and it corrects something this site had wrong.</b> At <b>Du Pont, Clinch County</b>, household 361: <i>Brown, James \u2014 Head \u2014 born September 1828 \u2014 aged 71 \u2014 married 35 years</i>, with his wife Sarah and four grown children. His birthplace, his father\u2019s and his mother\u2019s are all entered as <b>Georgia</b>.<br><br><b>That contradicts his daughter\u2019s death certificate, and the census wins.</b> When Kizzie Rice died in 1935 her certificate gave her father\u2019s birthplace as <b>North Carolina</b>, and this tree repeated it. But that answer came from a son-in-law, about a man who had been dead thirty-five years. The 1900 answer came from the household itself while James Brown was alive in it. <b>The North Carolina origin is withdrawn</b> \u2014 it is recorded here as a thing that was claimed and did not hold, because a tree that quietly deletes its mistakes is not worth more than the mistakes.<br><br><b>His marriage is gone, and now we know why.</b> Both he and Sarah are entered as <b>married 35 years</b>, which puts the wedding about <b>1865</b>. Clinch County\u2019s earliest surviving marriage book opens in <b>1867</b>; Berrien\u2019s covers 1860\u20131875 and its groom index holds exactly one Brown, Samuel, who married Sarah Hatchell. Ware and Echols have no books that reach back that far at all. The record is not lost through carelessness \u2014 the volume that held it no longer exists.<br><br><b>Eight children, and only five have names.</b> The 1900 census records Sarah as the mother of eight, all eight living. In the house that June were Kizzie (29), Crecy (22), Charley (20) and Mattie (15). Four more were grown and gone, and <b>Ida Frances, 26 and married to Charles Mercer in the same county, fits one of those four exactly</b> \u2014 by age, by county, and by the gap between Kizzie and Crecy. It is the strongest case this tree has for her without a record that names her outright.<br><br><b>One lead sits on the very next line of the page.</b> The household enumerated immediately after theirs is headed by <b>Harrison Hughes</b>, born September 1837 in Georgia \u2014 and <i>his</i> father and mother are both entered as born in <b>South Carolina</b>. Sarah was a Hughes. Neighbours with the wife\u2019s maiden surname, in a district this small, are rarely strangers.',
  src:[S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:M3NQ-1XD'),
       S.ga('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QJXM-K6TN'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/32978361/ida_frances-mercer')],
  cen:['c1870brown','c1900brown']},

sarahhughes:{n:'Sallie Hughes', g:'c', b:'Jan 1847', bp:'Clinch County, Georgia', d:'1904',
  ev:'<b>Two records, and they agree on her.</b> Her daughter Kizzie\u2019s 1935 death certificate gives the mother\u2019s maiden name as <b>Sallie Hughes</b>, born <b>Clinch County</b>. The 1900 census of Du Pont enters her as <b>Sarah</b>, wife, born <b>January 1847</b> in Georgia, her father and mother both Georgia-born. Sallie is the name she was called; Sarah is the name she was written down as.<br><br><b>She bore eight children and buried none of them before 1900</b> \u2014 the census records eight born and eight living, which in rural south Georgia in that generation is remarkable enough to be worth saying out loud. Four were still at home: Kizzie, Crecy, Charley and Mattie.<br><br><b>Married about 1865</b>, on the evidence of the census, when she was eighteen and the war had just ended.<br><br><b>She appears twice, under two versions of one name.</b> In 1850, in Ware County, she is <i>Sally Hughes, aged 4</i> — a line the index reads as “Lacey,” which is why no search for her ever found it. In 1860, in Clinch County, she is <i>Sarah M. Hughs, aged 13</i>. Sally is what the family called her; Sarah M. is what the register wanted; <b>Sallie Hughes</b> is the name her daughter’s death certificate gave in 1935. All one woman.<br><br><b>Hughes is a live thread now, not a dead one.</b> The family enumerated on the line directly below the Browns in 1900 is headed by <b>Harrison Hughes</b>, born September 1837 in Georgia, with a wife Rhoda and daughters Sallie, Carrie and Laura \u2014 and <b>both of his parents entered as born in South Carolina</b>. A Hughes household next door to a woman born Hughes, in a militia district of a few hundred people, is where this line goes next: the 1870 and 1880 censuses of Clinch County, looking for the two families side by side.',
  src:[S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:M3NQ-1XD'),
       S.ga('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QJXM-K6TN')],
  cen:['c1870brown','c1900brown']},

williamhughs:{n:'William H. Hughes', g:'c', b:'abt 1813', bp:'South Carolina',
  occ:['Farmer, Clinch County','pr',
    'The 1860 census enters him as <b>Farmer</b>, holding <b>$1,000</b> in real estate and <b>$355</b> in personal property — a landowner, and by some distance the richest man on this side of your tree at that date. For comparison, James Brown, who married his daughter, held two hundred dollars of personal property and no land at all when he was counted ten years later.'],
  ev:'<b>He is the reason there is a Carolina in your mother’s ancestry after all.</b> In the 1860 census of the <b>Southern District of Clinch County</b> — dwelling 253 — William Hughs, 45, and his wife Nancy, 43, are both entered as born in <b>South Carolina</b>, with eleven children under the same roof and every one of them born in <b>Georgia</b>. The family crossed the state line somewhere between about 1817 and about 1842.<br><br>That matters because this site spent a day believing the Carolina connection ran through the <i>Browns</i>, on the strength of a 1935 death certificate, and had to withdraw it when two censuses showed James Brown born in Georgia. The Carolina origin was real; it was simply on the other side of that marriage.<br><br><b>His fifth child is Sarah M. Hughs, aged 13</b> — born about 1847, the middle initial matching the <i>Sarah M.</i> on her own headstone, in the only household of that surname in the county. She married James Brown about 1865.<br><br><b>He is now confirmed, and the thing that confirmed him was a misread letter.</b> The 1850 census of <b>Ware County</b> — Clinch had been created that February and the enumerators had not caught up — holds the same household ten years younger: William H. Hughs, 37, born S. C., $500 in real estate; Nancy, 33, born S. C.; and five children, all born in Georgia. The fifth line is indexed as <i>Lacey</i>. At full resolution it reads <b>Sally</b>, aged four.<br><br><b>That completes a chain of four independent records.</b> <i>Sally</i> at four in 1850; <i>Sarah M.</i> at thirteen in 1860, the formal name for the same girl; <i>Sarah</i>, wife of James Brown, born January 1847, in the 1900 census of the same county; and <i>Sallie Hughes</i>, named as the mother on Kizzie Rice’s 1935 death certificate. Name, year and county agree across all four, and none of them copied from another. By the rule this tree runs on — multiple independent sources that agree — that is <b>confirmed</b>.',
  src:[S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MZMJ-BX6')],
  cen:['c1850hughes','c1860hughs']},

nancyhughs:{n:'Nancy Hughes', g:'c', b:'abt 1817', bp:'South Carolina',
  ev:'<b>Born in South Carolina about 1817</b>, and in June 1860 she was forty-three years old with <b>eleven children living in the house</b>, the eldest eighteen and the youngest one month. A twelfth, John, appears on the line above the household head.<br><br>Everything else about her is her husband’s record: the census gives her name, her age and her birthplace and nothing more — no maiden name, which is the usual wall for women of her generation. It would come from a Clinch or a South Carolina marriage record, or from a death certificate she was fifty years too early to have.<br><br>Her daughter <b>Sarah M.</b> was thirteen that summer and would marry James Brown about five years later, and bear eight children of her own.',
  src:[S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MZMJ-BX6')],
  cen:['c1850hughes','c1860hughs']},

lucius:{n:'Lucius L. Luke', g:'c', b:'9 Oct 1872', d:'21 Jun 1920', dp:'Ocilla, Irwin County',
  bur:'Brushy Creek Primitive Baptist Church Cemetery, Ocilla',
  occ:['Farmer','c',
    'Box 8 of his 1920 death certificate — “Trade, profession, or particular kind of work” — reads simply <b>Farmer</b>. The Fitzgerald paper was more generous: “a prominent farmer of Irwin county.”<br><br>The estate sales that followed say what that amounted to in equipment. <b>Four hundred and ninety acres</b> were advertised off the courthouse steps in 1922, levied again in 1923, and in December 1924 a creditor came for the machinery: a Frick Eclipse steam engine and boiler on iron wheels — a sawmill or threshing rig.'],
  ev:'<b>His death certificate is the keystone document of this tree.</b> Georgia certificate no. 18075 gives his birth as 9 October 1872 — correcting the 1873 in circulation — and names his own parents as <b>"R B Luke" and "Jane Yawn."</b> That is how this line reached back a further generation on documentary evidence rather than hearsay.<br><br><b>How he died is now known too.</b> The Fitzgerald paper, 23 June 1920: <i>"the remains of L. L. Luke, a prominent farmer of Irwin county were laid to rest at Brushy Creek church. The deceased was only sick for three days, when death overtook him."</i> He was 48.<br><br>His headstone reads <i>"An honest man, the noblest work of GOD"</i> beneath a Masonic emblem.<br><br><b>And then the family lost the land.</b> Within two years his widow Narcissus was levied on for unpaid taxes on <b>490 acres</b> — Lot 261, 5th land district — and again the year after that. In December 1924 a creditor took the family’s steam engine. The boll weevil had reached Georgia in 1915 and state cotton acreage halved between 1914 and 1923; the Lukes were inside a regional collapse, not merely unlucky.',
  src:[S.ga('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/gadeaths/id/39416'),
       S.np('https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn89053306/1920-06-23/ed-1/seq-5/'),
       S.np('https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn89053042/1922-06-09/ed-1/seq-10/'),
       S.np('https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn89053042/1924-12-12/ed-1/seq-7/')],
  cen:['c1900','c1910l']},

narcisus:{n:'Narcisus Harper', g:'c', b:'about 1877', d:'12 Nov 1940',
  bur:'Brushy Creek Primitive Baptist Church Cemetery, Ocilla',
  occ:['Administratrix of the estate','c',
    'No record found gives her a trade. What the record does give her is the job of holding the place together after 1920: the tax levies of 1922 and 1923 name <b>“Mrs. L. L. Luke”</b> as the party whose land was being sold, and the 1924 seizure of the steam engine names <b>“Narcissus Luke, administratrix”</b> of her husband’s estate.'],
  ev:'Named on two Georgia death certificates as the mother of Lucius Luke’s children — "Narcisus Harper" in 1919 and "Narcis Harper" in 1925. Married Lucius on <b>21 October 1894</b> per the Irwin County register as published in 1932. (Your notes said the 27th; the county register says the 21st.)<br><br><b>Her birth year needs correcting</b> — she is 22 in 1900 and 32 in 1910, both pointing to about <b>1877–78</b>, not the 1874 that circulates.<br><br><b>Her own parents are now identified: Henry S. Harper and Polly Vickers.</b> A 1932 county history lists the family and names "Narcissus, who married L. L. Luke" outright.<br><br>After Lucius died she was administratrix of an estate that was sold out from under her — 490 acres levied for taxes twice, and the steam engine seized in 1924.',
  src:[S.bk('https://archive.org/download/historyofirwinco00clem/historyofirwinco00clem_djvu.txt'),
       S.ga('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/gadeaths/id/34891'),
       S.mar('http://www.southgeorgiagenealogy.com/marrfix1.htm')],
  cen:['c1900','c1910l']},

mackie:{n:'Mackie Hinton', g:'c', b:'uncertain \u2014 see below', d:'about 1910', dp:'Levy County, Florida',
  ev:'<b>He is no longer a ghost \u2014 there is a marriage record.</b> The Levy County, Florida marriage licences read: <b>\u201cHinton, M. N. \u2014 Abby Johns \u2014 5 December 1901.\u201d</b> That is a document naming the couple, not an inference.<br><br>The 1910 census supports it from the other side: <b>Abby is enumerated as widowed</b>, confirming a husband dead before April 1910, and <b>the youngest child is a one-year-old boy named Mack.</b><br><br><b>His birth year is now genuinely in doubt, though.</b> An adult <b>M. N. Hinton sat on a Levy County jury in 1877</b> \u2014 so born by about 1856. The unsourced memorial giving Mackie as born 1880 cannot be that man, and the 1901 groom could be either. A search of all 62 archived Levy County cemetery transcriptions found <b>no Hinton headstone anywhere in the county</b>, which also undercuts that memorial\u2019s burial claim. The marriage return gives ages, and would settle it.',
  src:[S.mar('https://ufdcimages.uflib.ufl.edu/AA/00/06/43/30/00017/searchforyesterd1719levy_pdf.txt'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MVKD-BS6')], cen:['c1910h']},

abby:{n:'Abigail Elizabeth "Abby" Johns', g:'c', b:'about 1884', bp:'Florida',
  d:'3 Apr 1950', dp:'Levy County, Florida',
  ev:'<b>Confirmed as Nealie’s mother by the 1910 census</b> — which was the weakest link in the entire tree until that page turned up. She heads a Levyville household as a 26-year-old widow with four children, the middle one "Neal," aged two.<br><br><b>The Giddens remarriage is corroborated from an unexpected direction:</b> the 1930 Luke household in Irwin County contains <b>Ben Giddens, aged 11, born Florida</b> — Nealie’s half-brother, living with his grown sister. That anchors a cluster of online links that are otherwise internally contradictory.<br><br><b>And a claim about her own parents is now discredited.</b> The Henry Madison Johns / Margaret Douglas attribution fails its own test. The census says her father was born in <b>ALABAMA</b>; and no Henry Johns of any kind appears in any of the 62 archived Levy County cemetery transcriptions, in the county marriage lists 1854\u20131906, or in the Florida death index. The attribution has no documentary origin at all.<br><br>The <b>Douglas</b> half may survive \u2014 a Douglas family was demonstrably in Levy County in the right era. Her 1950 Florida death certificate would name both parents outright.<br><br><b>A third, independent corroboration of the Giddens remarriage turned up sideways.</b> Nealie\u2019s eldest sister proves to be <b>Montie Otelia Hinton</b>, who married John Henry Booth \u2014 and Booth is buried in the <b>Giddens family plot</b> at Chiefland Cemetery. That was reached with no reliance on any online tree.',
  src:[S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MVKD-BS6'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/15165074/abigail-elizabeth-giddens')],
  cen:['c1910h','c1930']},

george:{n:'George Albert "Art" McKinnon', g:'c', b:'about 1865', d:'9 Mar 1932',
  bur:'Arna Primitive Baptist Church Cemetery, Willacoochee, Atkinson County',
  ev:'<b>His death is now on a state record too.</b> Georgia’s death register for 1928–43 carries him as <b>Albert McKinnon</b>, died <b>1932</b> in <b>Militia District 748, Coffee County</b>, born about 1876 — the same district his daughter-in-law’s people were buried in, and the same one where Matilda Carver McKinnon died ten years later. The index carries no parents for him, so it does not reach a generation further back; the certificate image might.<br><br><b>Proven as Monroe’s father.</b> The 1910 census at Pearson lists him as "Art McKinnon," head of household, age 45, with Monroe among his sons. Until that page surfaced this was a plausible but unsourced claim.<br><br>His headstone gives May 1867; the census points to about 1865. Three of his other sons — William, Leon and Jimmie — are each independently confirmed as fathers in separate Coffee County obituaries, so the family group holds from both directions.<br><br>The McKinnons are named in the 1930 county history as among the members of <b>Arnie (Arna) Primitive Baptist Church</b>, organised about 1886 ten miles southwest of Douglas, "situated among a class of good farmers."',
  src:[S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MLKH-2Z4'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/33601515/albert-mckinnon'),
       S.bk('https://archive.org/details/wardshistoryofco00ward')], cen:['c1910m']},

martha:{n:'Martha "Mattie" Ricketson', g:'c', b:'about 1864–72', d:'12 Dec 1928',
  bur:'Arna Primitive Baptist Church Cemetery, Willacoochee, Atkinson County',
  ev:'<b>Her maiden name is proven from the census itself</b>, not from a tree. Her father <b>John Rickerson</b> was living in the household in 1910 and the enumerator labelled him "maternal grandfather."<br><br><b>Note the name correction.</b> Her headstone gives her as Martha <b>"Mattie"</b> Ricketson — <b>there is no middle name "Ann."</b> The "Martha Ann" in circulation appears unsourced; her own mother was Martha Amanda Overstreet, which may be where it came from.<br><br><b>An unresolved discrepancy:</b> the headstone says born 15 June 1864, but the census puts her at 38 in 1910, implying about 1872. Eight years apart. The relationship is settled; her birth year is not.',
  src:[S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MLKH-2Z4'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/33601555/martha-mckinnon')], cen:['c1910m']},

davidcribbs:{n:'David Benjamin Cribbs', g:'c', b:'about 1875', bp:'Georgia',
  occ:['Farmer','pr','Head of a farming household in Suwannee County, Florida in 1910 and at Pearson, Atkinson County by 1920.'],
  ev:'<b>Newly documented \u2014 and by two censuses rather than a memorial.</b> A second research file proposed him as Mattie Cribb\u2019s father on the strength of cemetery memorials and a compiled online profile, with the honest admission that the underlying records had never been opened. They have now.<br><br>The <b>1910 census of McAlpin, Suwannee County, Florida</b> enters <b>David B. Cribbs</b>, born about 1875 in <b>Georgia</b>, with his wife <b>Martha</b> and children <b>Mattie, Annie B. and Louis J.</b> The <b>1920 census of Pearson, Atkinson County, Georgia</b> has the same wife and the same children \u2014 Mattie, Annie B., Louis \u2014 with Lexie and Addie M. added, under a head the index reads as <i>\u201cDon Cribb.\u201d</i><br><br><b>That \u201cDon\u201d is almost certainly the indexer\u2019s reading of <i>D. B.</i></b> \u2014 initials written close together in a clerk\u2019s hand. The wife and three of the children carry across from one sheet to the other unchanged, which is what identifies the household; the head\u2019s name is the one thing that changes, and it changes in exactly the way a pair of initials does.<br><br><b>It also puts a second line of this family in Florida.</b> The Cribbses were in Suwannee County in 1910 and in Atkinson County by 1920 \u2014 the same Florida-to-south-Georgia crossing the Lukes and Hintons made from Levy County a decade later. Two of your four great-grandparent lines came north over that border.<br><br><i>Both households are so far read at the index level. The sheets themselves have not been opened, and the given name on the 1920 page is exactly the sort of thing that only the image settles.</i>',
  src:[], cen:[]},

marthacribbs:{n:'Martha "Mollie" Cribbs', g:'c', b:'about 1880',
  ev:'Wife of David Benjamin Cribbs and mother of Mattie. She is the fixed point that identifies the family across the two censuses: <b>Martha</b> in the 1910 Suwannee County household and <b>Martha</b> again in the 1920 Pearson household, with the same children around her both times, while the head\u2019s name shifts from <i>David B.</i> to <i>Don</i> between the two.<br><br>A compiled account gives her maiden name as <b>Crews</b> and the marriage as either <b>1896</b> or <b>20 October 1898</b> \u2014 two dates from the same body of derivative sources, which is the signature of a line copied between trees. Neither is adopted here. The marriage return would settle both the date and the surname.',
  src:[], cen:[]},

joshuacarver:{n:'Joshua Carver', g:'c', b:'about 1850', bp:'Georgia',
  ev:'<b>Elias\u2019s father, and a name this tree had guessed wrong.</b> The <b>1880 census of Militia District 1026, Coffee County</b> enters his household: <i>Joshua Carver, about 30</i>, his wife <b>Amanda</b>, and three children \u2014 <b>Elizer Jane</b>, <b>Elias</b> (three years old, born 1877) and <b>Leander</b>. 1880 is the first census that asked each person\u2019s relationship to the head of the house outright, so this is not an inference from who was sleeping where: it is the enumerator writing <i>son</i> against Elias\u2019s name.<br><br><b>His own father is not on the page, but his mother is \u2014 and her surname has now been corrected.</b> The sheet enters <b>Pheby</b>, aged 60, against the relationship <i>Mother</i>, and a <b>Mathew</b> of the same surname, aged 27, against <i>Brother</i>. The index reads that surname as <i>Sping</i>, and this page used to repeat it. <b>It is Spivey.</b> The <b>1860 census of the 4th District, 1030 Georgia Militia, Coffee County</b> holds <i>Phebia Spivy, 38</i>, with <b>Columbus, 15</b>, <b>Mathew, 8</b> and <b>Maranda, 1</b>. Mathew at eight in 1860 is Mathew at twenty-seven in 1880 \u2014 the same boy, twenty years apart, and the surname legible in the earlier hand. So Joshua\u2019s mother was <b>Pheby Spivey</b>, and his father was a Carver who was gone before she took a second husband\u2019s name.<br><br><b>The obituary’s “Joe Carver” is almost certainly not him.</b> Uncle Allen’s 1916 notice named his brothers as Jesse, Vincent, Joe and Pink, and the tempting move was to read <i>Joe</i> as short for Joshua. But a <b>Joseph Carver, born 17 August 1847 and died 16 January 1916</b>, lies buried in Coffee County — dying within a day of the notice that named him. That is the Joe of the list, and he is three years older than Joshua and a different man. With Pheby’s remarriage pointing the same way, <b>Joshua should not be hung on James Carver.</b><br><br><b>He is a boy of ten in 1860, in the middle of a Carver settlement.</b> The <b>1860 census of the 3rd District, 748 Georgia Militia, Coffee County</b> holds <i>Joshua Carver, aged 10</i> — and sixty-one other Carvers, nearly all in that one district, among them <b>Allen Carver, 15</b>, and <b>James Carver, 45</b>, the pair the 1916 obituary names as son and father. They were all neighbours. <b>That density is the whole problem with this surname in this county</b>, and it is why the 1860 page cannot be read from an index: that census recorded no relationships at all, only who slept under which roof.<br><br><b>But he did not grow up with her.</b> Two censuses put him somewhere else entirely, and in the same house both times. The <b>1870 census of Division 31, Coffee County</b>, household 211, page 35, enters <i>Joshua Carver, 20</i> on line 17 in the household of <b>John Carver, 50</b>, and <b>Martha Carver, 50</b>, alongside Margaret 22, Jesse 18, Maryan 16, Saryan 16, Martha 14, Eady 12, Elizer 11 and Emilia 9. The <b>1860 census of the 3rd District, 748 Georgia Militia</b> holds that same household ten years younger: <b>John, 40</b>, <b>Martha, 37</b>, Margaret 12, <i>Joshua, 10</i>, Jessee 8, Mary 6, Sary 6, Martha 4, Eady 2 and Elizia 1. <b>Eight names match across the two pages, aged exactly ten years.</b> Joshua sits in the middle of that birth order, two years under Margaret and two over Jesse \u2014 precisely where a son belongs.<br><br><b>And there is the difficulty, stated plainly: Martha cannot be his mother, because Pheby is.</b> Both readings that resolve it are worth writing down, because the tree should not pretend this is settled.<br><br><b>The first reading</b> takes the 1880 sheet at its word. Pheby Spivey is his mother; he was born to her in 1850 by a Carver; and he was raised among his father\u2019s people \u2014 in John Carver\u2019s house, with Martha, who would then be a stepmother or an aunt. On this reading <b>John Carver is very probably his father</b>, which is exactly why the boy was under his roof for twenty years, and Mathew Spivey is a half-brother.<br><br><b>The second reading</b> says the enumerator wrote <i>Mother</i> and <i>Brother</i> where he meant <i>mother-in-law</i> and <i>brother-in-law</i>, which happened often enough. It has one uncomfortably good piece of support: <b>Maranda Spivy, aged 1 in 1860, was born in 1859 \u2014 and Joshua\u2019s wife Amanda is 21 in 1880, born 1859.</b> If Amanda is Pheby\u2019s daughter Maranda, then Pheby is Joshua\u2019s mother-in-law, Mathew his brother-in-law, and Joshua is simply John and Martha\u2019s son.<br><br><b>The first reading is preferred here, and only just</b>, because it is the one an actual relationship column supports; the second rests on a shared birth year and a common name. <b>One page settles it.</b> The 1880 original for District 1026, sheet 238D, either says <i>Mother</i> or says <i>Mother in law</i>, and reading that column is the whole job. Until someone reads it, <b>Pheby is drawn to Joshua as probable, not confirmed</b>, and John and Martha Carver are named here but not drawn at all.',
  src:[], cen:[]},

phebyspivey:{n:'Pheby Spivey', g:'pr', b:'about 1820', bp:'Georgia',
  ev:'<b>Joshua Carver\u2019s mother, and the only woman of her generation on this branch with a name.</b> She is entered on the <b>1880 census of District 1026, Coffee County</b> in her son\u2019s house, aged 60, against the relationship <i>Mother</i>, with a <b>Mathew</b> of the same surname, 27, entered as <i>Brother</i> to the head.<br><br><b>The index calls her Sping. She is a Spivey.</b> The <b>1860 census of the 4th District, 1030 Georgia Militia, Coffee County</b>, page 35, holds <i>Phebia Spivy, 38</i>, with <b>Columbus, 15</b>, <b>Mathew, 8</b>, and <b>Maranda, 1</b>. Mathew is the same man who is 27 beside her twenty years later, which ties the two sheets together and fixes the spelling. No husband is entered with her in 1860 \u2014 she heads the household herself \u2014 so the Spivey she married was dead or gone by then.<br><br><b>Two surnames among her children is the whole story of her life.</b> Joshua was born a <b>Carver</b> in 1850; Mathew was born a <b>Spivey</b> about 1852. A first husband named Carver, dead or departed inside two years of Joshua\u2019s birth, and a second named Spivey, is the ordinary reading \u2014 and it is why Joshua\u2019s father has no name on any sheet that survives.<br><br><b>She is drawn to Joshua as probable rather than confirmed</b>, and the reason is set out on his page: the <b>Maranda</b> aged 1 in her 1860 household was born in 1859, and so was Joshua\u2019s wife Amanda. If those are the same woman, Pheby is his mother-in-law and not his mother. The 1880 original for sheet 238D says which, in one word, and nobody has yet read it.<br><br><b>Not to be confused with the Spiveys of Division 31</b>, where a <i>Mathew Spivy</i> of almost the same age lives with <b>John A. Spivy, 60</b>, and <b>Martha, 60</b>, in 1870. That is a different family in a different district, and it is not hers.',
  src:[], cen:[]},

amandacarver:{n:'Amanda Carver', g:'c', b:'about 1859',
  ev:'Wife of Joshua Carver and mother of Elias, entered beside them in the <b>1880 census of District 1026, Coffee County</b> with Elizer Jane and Leander.<br><br><b>Her maiden name is not on that sheet</b> \u2014 the census never asked \u2014 and no marriage return has been read for the couple. It would sit in the Coffee County marriage books, which the Georgia Archives has filmed: the same series that produced the Mercer\u2013Brown page and the McKinnon\u2013Carver licence already on this site.<br><br>Her daughter <b>Elizer Jane</b> stands beside an <b>Izer Jane</b> in the James J. Carver household in the same district. That is what Carver naming looks like in this county, and it is why same-name confusion runs right through this branch.<br><br><b>She is 21 on the 1880 sheet, not 25 \u2014 born about 1859, and this page has been corrected to match.</b> That year matters more than it looks. A <b>Maranda Spivy, aged 1</b>, appears in the 1860 household of <b>Phebia Spivy</b> of the 4th District, Coffee County \u2014 the same Pheby who is living with Joshua and Amanda twenty years later. <b>If Amanda is that child, the whole shape of the household changes</b>: Pheby becomes her mother rather than Joshua\u2019s, and Amanda\u2019s maiden name is <b>Spivey</b>. It is a birth year and a near-name, which is not proof of anything; it is set down because it is testable, and because the answer decides who Joshua\u2019s parents were. See his page for both readings.',
  src:[], cen:[]},

elias:{n:'Elias Carver', g:'c', b:'20 Mar 1877', bp:'Coffee County, Georgia', d:'13 Aug 1954',
  bur:'Carver Baptist Church Cemetery, Douglas, Coffee County',
  ev:'<b>The Carvers of Coffee County finally have a shape, though not yet a link to him.</b> The <i>Douglas Enterprise</i> of <b>15 January 1916</b> printed a full obituary for <b>“Uncle Allen Carver”</b>, about seventy, <i>“lived all his life in Coffee county”</i> — and it lays out three generations in one paragraph.<br><br>He was <b>a son of James Carver</b>, and brother to <b>Jesse, Vincent, Joe and Pink Carver</b>; his sisters were Mrs. Hulda Minix, Mrs. Boyce Gaskin, Mrs. Eliza Lott, Mrs. Mariah Davis, Mrs. Isaac Smith and Mrs. Sarah Purvis. He married <b>Miss Bede Vickers</b> and had seven children: Vincent, Jesse, Youngie, Mrs. Betsie Harper, Mrs. Mose Swilly, Mrs. Fannie Kirkland and Mrs. Henderson Day. He served in the <b>Fourth Georgia Regiment</b>.<br><br><b>His father is now known, and it is not one of the four this page proposed.</b> The <b>1880 census of Militia District 1026, Coffee County</b> has Elias at three years old in the household of <b>Joshua Carver</b> and his wife <b>Amanda</b>, with a sister Elizer Jane and a brother Leander. 1880 is the first census that recorded each person’s relationship to the head of the house, so the word <i>son</i> is the enumerator’s, not an inference.<br><br><b>The reasoning below was close and still wrong, and it is left standing rather than quietly deleted.</b> From Uncle Allen Carver’s obituary this page named <i>Jesse, Vincent, Joe or Pink</i> as the candidate fathers and <b>James Carver</b> as the candidate grandfather. Joshua is not among those four. He might be the <i>Joe</i> of the obituary — Joshua shortens that way — but nothing read here says so, and the 1880 sheet points the other direction: Joshua’s mother in that house is <b>Pheby</b>, under a second husband’s name, which is not what you would expect if he were James Carver’s son and James’s widow were still called Carver. <b>Treat the four-brother field as superseded.</b><br><br><b>Elias is not among those seven children</b>, so Allen is not his father. But Elias was born in Coffee County in 1877 to a Carver of precisely the generation of Allen’s brothers, which makes <b>Jesse, Vincent, Joe or Pink</b> the candidates and <b>James Carver</b> the candidate grandfather. That is a named field of four where there was nothing at all, and it is a <i>lead</i> — no record yet connects Elias to any of them.<br><br><b>The first documented facts about him, and they came free.</b> The Coffee County newspapers put Elias Carver in three places. In June <b>1912</b> he signs a public letter of thanks from the <b>patrons of the McClelland school</b>, alongside Dan Peterson, Bartley Vickers and Charlie Bowen. In March <b>1924</b> and again in <b>1926</b> he is <b>drawn as a juror</b> for the Superior Court of Coffee County.<br><br>Jury lists in Georgia in that period were drawn from men of standing and property, so this is the same kind of evidence that stands behind R. B. Luke’s entry: not a trade stated in words, but a place in the county’s civic life, recorded three times over twelve years.<br><br><b>Probable father of Mary Jane Carver — and the census makes the case strong.</b> The 1950 enumeration puts the Carver household a few doors from the McKinnons in the same district at Mora: Elias as head, Laura as wife aged 72, and daughter "Levada" aged 38, matching Mary Jane’s known sister Leevader Carver Starling. Her brother Ben Carver is enumerated nearby.<br><br><b>That missing record has now been found.</b> The <b>1920 census of Douglas, Coffee County</b> enters his household with his wife Laura and four children — <b>Mary J., Matilda, Levadar and Ben</b> — the same four this tree had assembled one at a time from the 1950 sheet and from neighbours’ households. And his daughter <b>Matilda’s 1942 Georgia death certificate</b> names him outright as her father. He is no longer a probable father; he is a documented one.<br><br><b>Be careful here</b> — there was more than one Carver–McKinnon marriage in this community, and two McKinnon grandchildren who are <i>not</i> Monroe’s were living in the Carver house in 1950.<br><br><b>He registered for the draft in Bacon County, not Coffee.</b> A <b>World War I registration card</b> for Elias Carver sits in <b>District 1729, Bacon County</b> — a county cut out of Appling, Pierce and Ware in 1914. The card gives his birthday as <b>20 March</b>, the same day and month cut on his stone, against a year of 1873 rather than 1877. Men rounded their ages at registration in both directions, and the day and month matching exactly is the stronger signal. It means that at some point between the 1900s and 1918 the family was living east of Coffee, and had come back to Douglas by 1920.<br><br><b>The year he married is the one year he cannot be found.</b> He married Laura in <b>December 1899</b>, and then: <b>no Elias Carver of his age appears anywhere in the 1900 census of the United States</b>, not in Coffee County and not in the state. Nor is there a Laura Carver in Coffee that year.<br><br><b>This page previously went further than that and was wrong.</b> It said there was not a single Story household in the whole county in 1900, and that the bride\u2019s family had dropped out of the index alongside the couple. <b>That was a search failing, not a family vanishing.</b> FamilySearch\u2019s 1900 collection silently returns nothing when a surname is given without a given name, and the negative was taken at face value. <b>The Storys were there the whole time.</b><br><br><b>Joseph Story, born January 1872, is at Militia District 748, Douglas town</b>, in the 1900 census — sheet 9B, household 171 — married eleven years, with his wife <b>Mary</b>, 26, and their children <b>Sarah 8</b>, <b>Ginney 5</b> and <b>Allen 2</b>. So the correction stands against this page rather than against the record: <b>Elias is the one missing from 1900, not his wife\u2019s people.</b>',
  src:[S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/35358790/elias-carver')], cen:[]},

laura:{n:'Laura Story', g:'pr', b:'5 Sep 1877', d:'11 Nov 1958',
  bur:'Carver Baptist Church Cemetery, Douglas, Coffee County',
  ev:'Married Elias Carver on <b>24 December 1899</b> in Coffee County. Read directly off the 1950 census page: "Carver, Laura — Wife — F 72 — Georgia." Her own parents are given as Josiah Story and Jincy Willis Story — <i>lead only</i>, from a memorial note rather than a record.<br><br><b>Her maiden name is now in dispute, and the better record is the one that disagrees with this page.</b> Her daughter <b>Matilda\u2019s 1942 Georgia death certificate</b> gives her mother as <b>Laura Newbern</b>. <i>Story</i> here comes from a cemetery memorial; <i>Newbern</i> comes from a state death certificate.<br><br>Neither is decisive on its own. The mother\u2019s-maiden-name box is the field informants get wrong more often than any other on a death certificate \u2014 Matilda\u2019s informant would have been her husband Leon McKinnon, reporting on his mother-in-law. But <i>Story</i> has never been read off a record either. <b>The marriage record has now been read, and it says Story.</b> Georgia’s county marriage index for Coffee County carries the entry directly: <i>Elias Carver — 24 December 1899 — Coffee, Georgia — Laura Story.</i> That is a county record taken at the time with the bride present, not a cemetery page, so <i>Story</i> is no longer resting on a memorial. It outranks a maiden name written down by a son-in-law forty-three years later on somebody else’s death certificate.<br><br><b>But she cannot be found as a child under either name, and that is worth saying plainly.</b> There is <b>no Laura Story born about 1877 anywhere in Georgia in the 1880 census</b> — not in Coffee County, not in the state — and <b>no Newbern or Newborn household in Coffee County in 1880 at all</b>. Nor is there a Story–Newbern marriage in the county, which kills the tidy explanation that she came to Elias as a young widow with a maiden name of Newbern.<br><br>So the position is this: one contemporaneous county record says <b>Story</b>, one later state record says <b>Newbern</b>, and <b>neither surname produces her in her own childhood household</b>. The likeliest readings are that her family was living outside Coffee County in 1880 and moved in later, or that she is indexed under a mangling of one of the two. <b>Story is preferred here on the strength of the marriage record; Newbern is kept on the page because a state certificate is not nothing.</b><br><br><b>Then the search was widened from her given name to her surname, and a household walked straight out of it.</b> There is exactly <b>one Story household in the whole of Coffee County in 1880</b>, and it is in <b>District 1026</b> — Elias\u2019s own district, the same page-range that holds his father Joshua. It is <b>Joseph Story, 35</b>, and <b>Jeney Story, 42</b>, with six children: Jacob 18, Cassy 14, Josiah 8, Jeney 5, <b>Marcenia, 3</b>, and Sarah C., 1.<br><br><b>Marcenia was born in 1877.</b> She is the only child of that year in the only Story family in the county, living in the district Elias grew up in and married out of.<br><br><b>And the name is not as far from Laura as it looks.</b> Elias\u2019s wife is written down four different ways across four records: <i>Laura</i> on the 1899 marriage return and again on the 1950 census, but <b><i>Larceny</i></b> in 1920, <b><i>Lucine</i></b> in 1930, and <b><i>Lareen</i></b> in the cemetery abstracts. Those last three are nobody\u2019s rendering of <i>Laura</i>. They are all reasonable manglings of something like <b>Larcenia</b> or <b>Lorena</b> — and <i>Larcenia</i> and <i>Marcenia</i> differ by one capital letter in a hand where L and M are routinely confused. A woman formally christened Larcenia, called Laura, would produce exactly this scatter of spellings.<br><br><b>The memorial turns out to have been working from something after all.</b> It gave her parents as <i>Josiah Story and Jincy Willis</i>. This household contains a <b>Josiah Story</b> — not the father but the brother, aged 8 — and the mother is entered as <b>Jeney</b>, which is how a clerk writes <b>Jincy</b>. The memorial appears to have taken a son\u2019s name for the father\u2019s and kept the mother\u2019s right. The actual father would be <b>Joseph</b>.<br><br><b>That reading has since softened in the memorial\u2019s favour.</b> The <i>Josiah Story</i> aged 8 on the 1880 sheet turns up in the <b>1900 census as <i>Joseph</i> Story</b>, head of his own house at Douglas town, born January 1872 — the same man, indexed under both names, and carried under both by FamilySearch. <b>This family used Joseph and Josiah interchangeably.</b><br><br><b>Which means the memorial may not have garbled anything at all.</b> If the father, Joseph Story born about 1845, was also known as Josiah, then <i>“Josiah Story”</i> is simply his name, and the memorial points straight at the head of the only Story household in the county. The naming runs on: that son called his own daughter <b>Ginney</b>, after the <b>Jeney</b> who is her grandmother on the 1880 page.<br><br><b>This is a lead, and it is deliberately not drawn on the tree.</b> What would settle it: her own <b>1958 death certificate</b>, which names both parents and can be ordered from the Georgia Archives; the <b>1900 census</b> for the Story household and for the newly married Carvers; or the name cut on her headstone. Until one of those is read, <b>Joseph and Jeney Story are named here and nowhere else.</b><br><br><b>One loose thread, noted rather than solved.</b> Jeney is <b>seven years older than Joseph</b>, and her eldest, Jacob, was born when Joseph was seventeen. That is the ordinary signature of a second marriage — an older widow with children, remarried to a younger man, her first family enumerated under his surname. If her first husband was a <b>Newbern</b>, the two competing maiden names on this page stop competing. That is a guess and is recorded as one.<br><br><b>And the Newberns have now been found in Coffee County, which this page had doubted.</b> It said there was no Newbern household in the county in 1880, and that remains what the 1880 census shows — but that is not the same as the family being absent from the county. <b>Thomas Newbern</b> swore an indigent pension application in <b>Coffee County on 3 May 1905</b>: born <b>17 January 1828 in Lowndes County</b>, a resident of Georgia all his life, <i>“farming when able”</i>, and supported in old age <i>“chiefly by help of children and friends.”</i> A second Newbern file, <b>E. D. Newbern</b>, sits beside his under the same county.<br><br><b>So there were Newberns in Coffee County, and they had children.</b> That does not make Laura one of them — no record joins her to Thomas — but it removes the objection that there were no Newberns there for her to be. The surname on Matilda\u2019s death certificate is not a phantom. <b>Thomas signed with a mark</b>, like most of the people on this page.<br><br>Story is a northern English name from the Old Norse personal name <i>Stori</i> — Scandinavian-derived, out of the Danelaw.',
  src:[S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/35358802/laura-carver')], cen:[]},

rb:{n:'Richard Benjamin "R. B." Luke', g:'c', b:'24 Dec 1853', bp:'Berrien County, Georgia',
  d:'2 Aug 1926', dp:'Coffee County, Georgia', bur:'Luke Cemetery, Coffee County',
  occ:['Farmer and landowner','pr',
    'No document states his trade in a single word. What they state is land. In 1914 his holdings near Lax bounded a neighbour’s 245 acres on two sides, and he was drawn as a juror for the City Court of Douglas — a list drawn in that period from freeholders. He died intestate and his widow Jane was appointed administratrix. A Masonic emblem is cut on his stone.'],
  ev:'<b>The lost daughter now has a place and a child.</b> The <i>Coffee County Progress</i> of <b>19 May 1915</b>: <i>“Mrs. W. M. Smith, and daughter, Miss <b>Levada</b>, of the <b>Rocky Pond section</b>, were in the city Thursday shopping.”</i> So R. B. and Jane’s daughter lived at Rocky Pond and had a daughter named Levada.<br><br><b>And an unresolved coincidence, recorded as nothing more than that.</b> Eight years earlier the same paper carried guardianship notices for a <b>Levada Harper, a minor and a ward</b>, who owned land in the Fifth District of Coffee County <i>bounded on the east by the land of Henry Harper</i>. A Levada who is a landowning orphan in 1907 and a Levada living with Mrs. W. M. Smith in 1915 would fit a plain story — a first marriage to a Harper, a death, a remarriage — and it would tie the Lukes to the Harpers a second time. <b>It would also fit two unrelated girls sharing an uncommon local name.</b> Nothing here decides between those, and the Irwin register carries no Harper—Luke marriage. It is a thread, not a finding.<br><br><b>A daughter nobody had.</b> The <i>Douglas Enterprise</i> of <b>8 June 1912</b>, in the Rocky Pond column, prints a single sentence worth a census page: <i>“Mrs. R. B. Luke spent several days with her daughter <b>Mrs. W. M. Smith</b>.”</i> Two lines above, the same column reports that Mrs. W. M. Smith and her children had been ill with measles. So R. B. and Jane had a daughter who married a <b>W. M. Smith</b> of Douglas and was raising children by 1912 — a child of this couple who appears nowhere else in this file.<br><br>She is in the reunion notice too. The guest list at <b>R. B. Luke’s house near Lax in July 1914</b> reads: <i>Mr. R. Smith and family, Douglas; Mr. E. F. Vickers and family, Ambrose; Mr. W. M. Smith and family, Douglas; Messrs. O. G. Luke, G. G. Luke, Elisha Paulk, Ocilla; Mr. Jas. Luke and wife, Alapaha; Mr. Mar[cus] Luke and family, Alapaha; Mr. Arthur Luke and family, Willacoochee.</i> Every one of those households is a thread nobody has pulled.<br><br><b>A note worth keeping in view rather than acting on:</b> Jane’s own mother was a <b>Smith</b>, and her daughter married a Smith. Smith is the commonest surname in the county and this proves nothing — but if the Yawn question is ever cracked, it may be cracked from the Smith side.<br><br><b>He got into this tree on a document.</b> Lucius Luke’s 1920 death certificate names his father plainly as "R B Luke," and the 1860 and 1870 Berrien County censuses show him as a boy of 6 and a young man of 17 in John Luke’s house.<br><br><b>He was a substantial landowner near Lax.</b> Tax-sale notices for a neighbour’s 245 acres in 1924–25 describe the tract as "bounded on the North by lands of R. B. Luke… on the West by lands of R. B. Luke and O. J. Paulk" — he owned land on two sides of it, sixteen months before he died. He was drawn as a juror for the City Court of Douglas in 1914.<br><br><b>And he held big family reunions.</b> The <i>Douglas Enterprise</i> of 18 July 1914 reported one at his residence near Lax, listing guests from Douglas, Ambrose, Ocilla, Alapaha and Willacoochee — one paragraph that maps the whole clan’s geography. Another followed in 1915, ending with "a prayer and praise meeting."<br><br>He died <b>intestate</b>: in September 1926 "Jane Luke having made application in due form of law to be appointed Administratrix upon the estate of R. B. Luke." His headstone carries a Masonic emblem.<br><br>He also resolves the <b>John J. Luke question</b> — that older John J. Luke (1876–1939) was his son, which makes him Jack’s uncle, not his father.',
  src:[S.ga('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/gadeaths/id/39416'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/35224777/richard-benjamin-luke'),
       S.np('https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn89053042/1914-07-18/ed-1/seq-1/'),
       S.np('https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn89053042/1926-09-10/ed-1/seq-7/')],
  cen:['c1860b','c1870b','c1880rb']},

janeyawn:{n:'Jane (Janie Ruth) Yawn', g:'c', b:'14 Mar 1855', bp:'<b>Coffee County, Georgia</b>', d:'1 Nov 1943',
  occ:['Administratrix','c',
    'Appointed administratrix of R. B.’s intestate estate — which is to say the county put the settling of it in her hands. She outlived him by roughly eighteen years.'],
  ev:'<b>A daughter nobody had.</b> The <i>Douglas Enterprise</i> of <b>8 June 1912</b>, in the Rocky Pond column, prints a single sentence worth a census page: <i>“Mrs. R. B. Luke spent several days with her daughter <b>Mrs. W. M. Smith</b>.”</i> Two lines above, the same column reports that Mrs. W. M. Smith and her children had been ill with measles. So R. B. and Jane had a daughter who married a <b>W. M. Smith</b> of Douglas and was raising children by 1912 — a child of this couple who appears nowhere else in this file.<br><br>She is in the reunion notice too. The guest list at <b>R. B. Luke’s house near Lax in July 1914</b> reads: <i>Mr. R. Smith and family, Douglas; Mr. E. F. Vickers and family, Ambrose; Mr. W. M. Smith and family, Douglas; Messrs. O. G. Luke, G. G. Luke, Elisha Paulk, Ocilla; Mr. Jas. Luke and wife, Alapaha; Mr. Mar[cus] Luke and family, Alapaha; Mr. Arthur Luke and family, Willacoochee.</i> Every one of those households is a thread nobody has pulled.<br><br><b>A note worth keeping in view rather than acting on:</b> Jane’s own mother was a <b>Smith</b>, and her daughter married a Smith. Smith is the commonest surname in the county and this proves nothing — but if the Yawn question is ever cracked, it may be cracked from the Smith side.<br><br><b>Named on Lucius Luke\u2019s 1920 death certificate as his mother: \u201cJane Yawn.\u201d</b> That certificate, read from the original image rather than the index, also gives her birthplace \u2014 <b>Coffee County, Georgia</b> \u2014 and that one word is what made everything below possible.<br><br><b>She has now been found as a child, and the record is a hard one.</b> The 1860 census of Coffee County, 5th District, P.O. Bird\u2019s Mills, page 527, lists three small Yawn girls boarded in a neighbour\u2019s house \u2014 the Elias Johns family \u2014 with no parents present: <b>Elizabeth, 5 \u00b7 Jane, 4 \u00b7 Pollyan, 1.</b> Two doors away live the only other Yawns in the county, Shadrach, 96, born South Carolina, and his wife Rosanah, 78.<br><br>The mortality schedule for the same county and year explains why the girls were boarded out: <b>\u201cElizabeth Yawn, 29, married, born Georgia, died February 1860 \u2014 cause of death: Child Bed.\u201d</b> Their mother died in childbirth four months before the census. Polly Ann, aged one, was almost certainly the child she died having.<br><br><b>And it makes the sisters real.</b> Elizabeth Luke n\u00e9e Yawn \u2014 who died in Clinch County in 1924 with a certificate naming her father as \u201cYawn\u201d and her mother as \u201cSmith\u201d \u2014 is that five-year-old. She and Jane were <b>sisters</b>, not a coincidence of two Yawn women marrying two Luke men. The \u201cAntney Yawn\u201d parentage now rests on a household, not a name match \u2014 stronger, but still short of proof.<br><br>She outlived her husband by seventeen years and was appointed administratrix of his intestate estate in 1926. <b>There is no Yawn of any spelling in the 1870 Coffee County census</b> \u2014 the family had gone.',
  src:[S.ga('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/gadeaths/id/39416'),
       S.cen('https://iiif.archive.org/iiif/populationschedu117unit%24530/full/full/0/default.jpg'),
       S.bk('http://genealogytrails.com/geo/coffee/1860-mortality.html')], cen:['c1880rb']},

henrysharper:{n:'Henry S. Harper', g:'c', b:'about 1854',
  ev:'<b>Narcisus Harper’s father — newly identified.</b> A 1932 history of Irwin County lists the family outright: "Henry S. Harper married Polly Vickers. Children: Asa… Richard… Dawson… Edwin… Sallie… <b>Narcissus, who married L. L. Luke</b>, and Betty."<br><br>He is corroborated independently in the 1870 census as <b>"Henry S., 16"</b> in the household of Henry S. C. Harper and Nancy at Irwinville — which places him in the right family at the right age.',
  src:[S.bk('https://archive.org/download/historyofirwinco00clem/historyofirwinco00clem_djvu.txt'),
       S.fs('http://us-census.org/pub/usgenweb/census/ga/irwin/1870/pg0269a.txt')], cen:[]},

pollyvickers:{n:'Polly Vickers', g:'c',
  ev:'Named as Narcisus Harper’s mother in the 1932 Irwin County history. The Vickers were one of the county’s pioneer families and the name recurs throughout these records — including among the guests at R. B. Luke’s 1914 reunion.<br><br><b>One of those Vickers left a soldier\u2019s record worth setting down, though he is not yet joined to her.</b> <b>William Vickers of Douglas</b> applied for a Georgia pension on <b>29 August 1915</b>. He enlisted at <b>Savannah in April 1862</b> in <b>Company A, 61st Georgia</b>, served <i>“till the war closed,”</i> and gave the place of his surrender as <b>Appomattox, April 9th 1865</b>. Asked when he left his command he answered in three words: <b><i>“Did not leave.”</i></b><br><br><b>Three years from Savannah to Appomattox without once being absent</b> is a rare answer on these forms, where most men record capture, furlough or sickness. <b>No record yet joins him to Polly</b>, and he is named here as part of the Coffee County Vickers rather than as her kin.',
  src:[S.bk('https://archive.org/download/historyofirwinco00clem/historyofirwinco00clem_djvu.txt')], cen:[]},

jimmckinnon:{n:'James "Jim" McKinnon', g:'pr', b:'9 Dec 1846', bp:'Waresboro, Ware County, Georgia',
  d:'6 Sep 1924', dp:'Coffee County', bur:'Arna Primitive Baptist Church Cemetery',
  occ:['Farmer and landowner','c',
    'Farmed in the 6th District, Land Lot 202. One of the founding families of Roberts’ Methodist Church. He is remembered as having read nothing but the Bible and the hymn book.'],
  ev:'Married Mary Jane Durham in 1863; thirteen children, of whom George Albert is yours. A landowner in the 6th District, Land Lot 202, with sons and kin holding the tracts around him.<br><br><b>The best single document in this whole project is about him.</b> The <i>Douglas Enterprise</i> of 13 July 1912 described a Fourth of July reunion at his house — a melon cutting, "a barrel of Lemonade setting in the gate with about a dozen dippers that meant help yourself," dinner "spread out under the large oaks on a long table," and a crop "such as dont grow on every mans place." The correspondent ends: <i>"some one will have to read this little notice for uncle Jim for he never reads news papers nor anything else but the bible or hymn book."</i><br><br>Seven years later the same paper noted that <b>Mrs. McKinnon called at the office and renewed her subscription</b>. She was the reader in that marriage.<br><br>He and the Durhams were among the founding families of Roberts’ Methodist Church, organised about 1866 near Kirkland.',
  src:[S.np('https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn89053042/1912-07-13/ed-1/seq-1/'),
       S.bk('https://archive.org/details/wardshistoryofco00ward'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/33601533/james-mckinnon')], cen:[]},

marydurham:{n:'Mary Jane Durham', g:'pr', b:'25 Sep 1844', d:'1921',
  ev:'Married Jim McKinnon in 1863 and bore thirteen children. Her people, the Durhams, helped found Roberts’ Methodist Church alongside the McKinnons about 1866 — Seth Durham of Ware County, born in South Carolina about 1799, sat on the first Coffee County grand jury in 1854.<br><br>In January 1919 the <i>Douglas Enterprise</i> recorded that she came into the office and <b>renewed the family’s newspaper subscription in her own name</b> — a small thing that says a great deal, given that her husband could read nothing but the Bible and the hymn book.<br><br><b>A caution:</b> the published sketch naming Seth Durham and Elzie Taff as her parents does not survive checking — Seth Durham’s 1850 household contains no Mary Jane. The McKinnon–Durham association is solid; her specific parentage is not.',
  src:[S.np('https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn89053042/1919-01-17/ed-1/seq-5/'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/33601551/mary-jane-mckinnon')], cen:[]},

rickerson:{n:'John P. Ricketson', g:'c', b:'4 Jun 1828', bp:'Montgomery County, Georgia',
  d:'2 Jul 1912', dp:'Coffee County', bur:'Royals Cemetery, Kirkland, Atkinson County',
  occ:['Farmer','c',
    'Asked his occupation on his Confederate pension application he answered, under oath, <b>Farmer</b>, and signed the paper with a mark. He served in <b>Company I, 26th Georgia</b>.<br><br>The rest of the file is harder than that one word. His widow’s affidavit, sworn at Douglas on 5 August 1912, records that he had been drawing the state’s <b>indigent</b> pension — sixty dollars for the year — and that at his death he was in the use and possession of, in the clerk’s hand, <b>no property</b>.'],
  ev:'<b>He is here because a census enumerator wrote three words.</b> In 1910, aged 86, he was living in his daughter’s house at Pearson, and the enumerator recorded his relationship as <b>"maternal grandfather"</b> — which is what turns the Ricketson name from a tree claim into a documented fact.<br><br><b>And we have his own sworn words.</b> His Confederate pension file contains three affidavits, each signed <b>with his mark</b> — he could not write his name. Asked where he was born, he answered <i>"Montgomery County, Ga."</i> He enlisted <b>12 January 1862, Company I, 26th Regiment Georgia Volunteers</b>; was <b>captured and carried to Point Lookout, Maryland</b>; and was <b>wounded four times and never recovered</b>. In 1902 he swore he had lived in Georgia continuously "ever since the 4 day of June 1828."<br><br>Take that date over the 1830 on his memorial and the 1824 implied by the census — it is the only version he stated under oath, and he stated it twice.<br><br><b>The Peterson claim is refuted as stated.</b> A note on his memorial had him as the son of Sarah Ricketson and a John Peterson, born before she married. Huxford confirms Sarah Ricketson married James Carver — but <b>Sarah was born in 1820</b>, and John P. swore he was born in 1828. She would have been eight years old. The kernel of the story is real, though: Sarah\u2019s sister <b>Rachel Ricketson married James Peterson on 24 January 1825</b>.<br><br><b>Where he probably belongs.</b> Huxford sketches <b>Joseph Ricketson (1784\u20131820) of Montgomery County</b>, son of Timothy Ricketson Jr. and grandson of <b>Timothy Ricketson Sr., a Revolutionary soldier</b> granted 287\u00bd acres of bounty land in Washington County on 20 November 1784. After Joseph died his widow Serena moved to Ware County and farmed in the <b>586th Militia District</b> — the very district where \u201cKINON, James M.\u201d was enumerated in 1840. <b>The Ricketsons and the McKinnons were neighbours a generation before they married.</b> John P. is most likely a grandson of Joseph through one of his adult sons; that link is not yet proven.<br><br><b>Do not merge him with the other John.</b> Huxford also sketches a John Ricketson 1819\u20131891, and Ward\u2019s 1854 Coffee County petit jury list names John Ricketson, John P. Ricketson and Benjamin Ricketson as three separate men.<br><br><b>His own pension application has now been read, fourteen years before his widow\u2019s.</b> On <b>17 January 1898</b> he swore it at Douglas before the Ordinary of Coffee County, and it answers in his own words several things this tree had only by inference.<br><br><b>Born in Montgomery County, Georgia</b> — sworn, not deduced — and a resident of the state <i>“during life.”</i> He <b>enlisted on 12 January 1862 in Company I, 26th Regiment Georgia Volunteers</b> and stayed <i>“during war.”</i> Asked how he left the service he wrote: <b><i>“Captured, carried to Point Lookout in M.D.”</i></b> — the Union prison camp on the Maryland shore.<br><br><b>And then the line that explains the rest of his life.</b> Asked why he could not support himself he answered: <b><i>“Ten years old age. Having been wounded four times and never did recover from wounds.”</i></b> Occupation since 1865: <i>“no particular occupation.”</i> Property: <i>“I have nothing.”</i> How he was supported in 1896 and 1897: <i>“small amount by labor, the rest charity of neighbors.”</i> Family: <i>“myself and wife … we have no homestead.”</i><br><br><b>He signed it with a mark</b>, as Amanda would sign hers in 1912. The regiment on both papers is the same — Company I, 26th Georgia — which is what ties her affidavit to his application beyond the shared surname.',
  src:[S.pen('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/TestApps/id/307550'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MLKH-2Z4'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/72551977/john_p_griffis-ricketson')], cen:['c1910m']},

marthaoverstreet:{n:'Martha Amanda Overstreet', g:'pr', b:'about 1835', d:'about 1921',
  occ:['No property of her own','c',
    'Her widow’s affidavit lists what she held, item by item, and enters nothing against any of it: acres of land, <b>“No”</b>; horses and mules, none; hogs and cows, none; total cash value, <b>0</b>. She signed it “Amanda <span style=\'font-family:monospace\'>×</span> Ricketson, her mark,” and swore she had lived in Coffee County continuously since <b>1843</b>.'],
  ev:'<b>Her marriage is now proven; her maiden name still is not.</b> Those are two different questions and the record answers only one of them.<br><br>Her own <b>widow’s pension affidavit</b>, sworn before the Ordinary of Coffee County on <b>5 August 1912</b>, opens: <i>“Personally before me comes <b>Amanda Ricketson</b> of said County, who, after being duly sworn, on oath says that she is the widow of <b>J. P. Ricketson</b>”</i> — married in Coffee County, resident there at his death, not since remarried. That is a primary record, and it settles that John P. Ricketson’s widow was named Amanda and was living at Douglas in 1912.<br><br><b>What it does not do is prove Overstreet.</b> The affidavit gives her married name only. The surname Overstreet reaches this tree from an unsourced memorial and stays <i>lead only</i> until a marriage record or a death certificate names her father. She signed the affidavit with a mark and swore she had lived in the county continuously since <b>1843</b>, which puts her in that corner of Georgia a decade before Coffee County existed.<br><br>The surname is interesting in its own right: <b>Overstreet is probably not English at all</b>, but an altered form of the Flemish <i>Overstraete</i> — topographic, roughly "across the street." It is absent from the British surname dictionaries. Flemish and Dutch families entered the Chesapeake and the Carolinas in the colonial period, which fits the standard Virginia → Carolinas → Georgia route exactly.<br><br><b>And an Overstreet of the right county left a pension file, though nothing yet joins him to her.</b> <b>E. W. Overstreet of Nashville, Berrien County</b>, applied on <b>13 October 1919</b>. He enlisted at <b>Savannah in April 1864</b>, and eight months later the war took him in one stroke: he left his command on <b>21 December 1864</b> — <i>“was captured”</i>, by the authority of <i>“the enemy”</i> — and spent the rest of it <b>in prison at Point Lookout</b>, released <b>June 1865</b>.<br><br><b>21 December 1864 is the day Savannah fell to Sherman.</b> He was taken with the city.<br><br><b>Point Lookout is where this tree has been before.</b> <b>John P. Ricketson</b> — the man who married an Overstreet — answered the same question on his own application with <i>“Captured, carried to Point Lookout in M.D.”</i> Two men from these families in the same Maryland prison camp. <b>It proves no kinship</b>, and her surname still rests where it rested; but if the Overstreets and the Ricketsons were already connected before that marriage, this is the sort of trace it would leave. <b>E. W. signed his name in his own hand</b>, which on these pages is rarer than not.',
  src:[S.pen('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/TestApps/id/297716')], cen:[]},

johnluke:{n:'John Luke', g:'c', b:'about 1798', d:'10 Oct 1875',
  bur:'St. Luke Missionary Baptist Church Cemetery, Alapaha, Berrien County',
  occ:['Farmer','c',
    'The 1860 census of Berrien County calls him a <b>farmer</b> and puts a figure on it: $500 in land, $760 in personal property — which in that county in that year meant mostly livestock running in unfenced pine woods. He and Telitha are both marked unable to read or write.'],
  ev:'<b>Proven as R. B. Luke’s father by two censuses</b> — 1860, with Richard B. aged 6, and 1870, with him aged 17. The "implausible 32-year age gap" that hung over this couple turns out to be an artifact of a bad memorial birth year: the census gives 62 against 40, a 22-year gap, and <b>it was his second marriage</b>.<br><br>His first wife was an Elizabeth whose surname is unknown; Joseph, John G., James E., Elizabeth C. and William H. are hers. He married <b>Telitha Jane Graves on 11 August 1850 in Laurens County</b>, where her family had just arrived from North Carolina. Ruth J. (1852) and Richard B. (1853) begin her children.<br><br>There is a lovely internal corroboration here: <b>two North Carolina–born Graves sisters married a father and his son.</b> Telitha married John; her sister Rutha Ellen married John’s son James E. The 1860 census shows them in adjacent dwellings, both women recorded as born in North Carolina.<br><br>He farmed, owned $500 in land and $760 in personal property — mostly livestock — and <b>he and Telitha were both unable to read or write</b>. <b>He held no enslaved people</b>: the Berrien County slave schedule was read page by page and no Luke appears on it.<br><br><b>Whether he was Daniel Luke’s son is unresolved.</b> No document links them, and the Irwin County marriage register for 1820–1835 and the tax digests for 1820–1830 are both lost.',
  src:[S.fs('http://us-census.org/pub/usgenweb/census/ga/berrien/1860/pg00363.txt'),
       S.fs('https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MCSF-B51'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/38336202/john-luke')],
  cen:['c1860b','c1870b']},

telitha:{n:'Telitha Jane Graves', g:'c', b:'10 Jun 1819', bp:'<b>North Carolina</b>', d:'16 Nov 1896',
  bur:'St. Luke Missionary Baptist Church Cemetery, Alapaha, Berrien County',
  ev:'<b>Confirmed, and she carries the tree out of Georgia.</b> Both the 1860 and 1870 censuses record her as born in <b>North Carolina</b> — the only foreign-state birth in the direct Luke line for three generations.<br><br>She married John Luke on <b>11 August 1850 in Laurens County, Georgia</b>, where the Graves family had just arrived. Her sister Rutha Ellen married John’s son James E., and the 1860 census shows the two North Carolina–born sisters living in adjacent houses.<br><br>Her people trace to <b>Brunswick County, North Carolina</b>, where Benjamin Graves appears in the 1790 census, born before 1774. Her parents are given as Joseph Graves (b. about 1796, NC) and Rutheo Ogletree (b. about 1793, NC).<br><br>Like her husband, she was <b>unable to read or write</b>. Her granddaughter through Jemima Patience was named <b>Tillitha Jane Harper</b> for her.',
  src:[S.fs('http://us-census.org/pub/usgenweb/census/ga/berrien/1860/pg00363.txt'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/38336658/tetlita-jane-luke')],
  cen:['c1860b','c1870b']},

hscharper:{n:'Henry S. C. Harper', g:'c', b:'about 1817', bp:'<b>Irwin County, Georgia</b>',
  ev:'<b>Born in Irwin County itself.</b> Two of his sons’ death certificates, filed two years apart by two different informants, both give his birthplace as <b>Irwin Co., Ga.</b> He was born about 1817 and the county was created in <b>1818</b> — so the Harpers were on that ground from the county’s first year, earlier than the Lukes, who do not appear there until the 1820s.<br><br>Narcisus Harper’s grandfather. The 1870 census finds him at Irwinville, aged 53, a farmer with $500 in land and $1,810 in personal property, his wife Nancy beside him and seven children at home including <b>"Henry S., 16"</b> — Narcisus’s father.<br><br>In 1860 he was better off still: <b>$1,400 in land and $2,235 in personal property</b>. <b>And he is absent from the Irwin County slave schedule for both 1850 and 1860.</b><br><br><b>That is not the same as clean, and this page used to let it stand for clean.</b> <b>His father’s will left him a person.</b> Leonard Harper, dictating on 29 June 1842: <i>“Second I give and bequeath unto my son <b>Henry S. C. Harper</b> and <b>Fleming B. Harper</b> a negro boy named <b>Tom</b>.”</i> Henry was about twenty-five. What became of Tom between 1842 and the 1860 schedule — sold, died, or passed back into his mother’s household, where two men of the right age were still held — is not on any record yet found. The schedule and the will are both true, and the will is the older and the plainer of the two.<br><br>That pairing is the single most useful lesson in this whole archive. In the same county the same year, Susan Harper held $2,485 in personal property <i>and</i> two enslaved men. Nearly identical estates, opposite records. In wiregrass Georgia personal property meant <b>cattle</b> — herds of several hundred head were ordinary. <b>Property value is not a proxy for slaveholding in either direction.</b><br><br>His land fell from $1,400 to $500 between 1860 and 1870. Since he held no one in 1860, that decline is wartime loss — impressment, currency collapse, devalued land — not emancipation.',
  src:[S.fs('http://us-census.org/pub/usgenweb/census/ga/irwin/1870/pg0269a.txt'),
       S.sl('http://us-census.org/pub/usgenweb/census/ga/irwin/1860/slave.txt')], cen:[]},

nancymerritt:{n:'Nancy Merritt', g:'c', b:'about 1817', bp:'<b>North Carolina</b>',
  ev:'<b>North Carolina, corroborated — and contradicted.</b> The 1870 census at Irwinville records her as Nancy, 53, <b>born North Carolina</b>. Her son <b>George J. Harper</b>’s 1924 death certificate says the same thing independently, fifty-four years later: maiden name <i>Nancy Merritt</i>, birthplace <b>N.C.</b><br><br>But her son <b>Luke L. Harper</b>’s certificate, filed in 1926 by a different informant, names the same parents and then gives her birthplace as <b>Irwin Co., Ga.</b> Two of three say North Carolina, and it is the harder answer to invent — an informant guessing reaches for the county he is standing in, not a state five hundred miles away. But the disagreement is on the record and belongs here.<br><br>The transcriber of the 1870 sheet notes her as née Nancy Anna Merritt. She is the second North Carolina birth in this tree, alongside Telitha Jane Graves, and both point the family back toward the Carolinas a generation before Georgia.<br><br>A 1932 county history lists the couple’s <b>thirteen</b> children. Four of them can now be named from documents rather than from the book: George J. (1842—1924), Luke L. (about 1845—1926), Flem B. (about 1849—1923), and Henry S., who married Polly Vickers and fathered Narcisus. Turn on <i>Show children</i> to see them.',
  src:[S.fs('http://us-census.org/pub/usgenweb/census/ga/irwin/1870/pg0269a.txt'),
       S.bk('https://archive.org/download/historyofirwinco00clem/historyofirwinco00clem_djvu.txt'),
       S.ga('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/gadeaths/id/215283'),
       S.ga('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/gadeaths/id/289491')], cen:[]},

jamesmalcolm:{n:'James Malcolm McKinnon Sr.', g:'pr', b:'about 1804', bp:'<b>North Carolina</b>',
  d:'1880', dp:'Coffee County, Georgia',
  occ:['Sheriff of Coffee County','c',
    'A militia private in 1838, in the companies raised for the removal of the Cherokee; then <b>Sheriff of Coffee County, 1856–1858</b>.<br><br>The office is what puts the hardest page in this file in his hands. A Georgia sheriff in that decade conducted the sales that settled debts and estates, and in this state in that decade the property sold at the courthouse door included people.'],
  ev:'<b>The deepest McKinnon ancestor, and the most morally complicated person in this tree.</b><br><br>He served as a private in <b>Captain Levi J. Knight’s Independent Company, Lowndes County, 15 August – 15 October 1838</b>, raised, in the muster roll’s own words, "on a sudden emergency to repel the invasion of the Indians." 1838 is the year of the Cherokee removal; in south Georgia it was the aftermath of the Creek war and the tail of the Second Seminole War. Settlers were in real danger and experienced it as defence. It was also, unmistakably, service on the removal side of a war of dispossession, on land already being handed out to white settlers by lottery. Both things are true and the second does not cancel the first.<br><br>He was <b>Sheriff of Coffee County, 1856–1858</b> — and in that office he conducted <b>sales of enslaved people at the courthouse door</b>. The notices survive: "One negro man, Backus, about thirty-five years old"; "One negro woman, Ar’sey, 24 or 25 years old, and her two children."<br><br>He appears in the 1840 Ware County census as <b>"KINON, James M."</b> — the odd spelling is why he is easy to miss. He married three times: Nancy Atkinson, Emily "Millie" Rodgers of Tattnall County, and Louisa Meeks of Emanuel County.<br><br><b>His own parents are unknown.</b> No record names them, no North Carolina county is identified, and the line stops here.<br><br><b>A warning about the name itself.</b> Huxford\u2019s index to <i>Pioneers of Wiregrass Georgia</i> carries <b>two</b> McKinnons with identical 1804\u20131880 dates: \u201cMcKINNON, James, Atkinson, Vol. VI\u201d and \u201cMcKINNON, Malcolm, Echols, Vol. IV.\u201d The second is a demonstrably different man \u2014 Thomas then Brooks then Echols County, Methodist, Echols County Treasurer, married Margaret McKinnon in 1832. <b>The composite \u201cJames Malcolm McKinnon\u201d may be a conflation of the two.</b> Volume VI has never been digitised; until someone reads it, treat the middle name as unverified.<br><br><b>And one argument against Highland descent does not survive.</b> Huxford shows the Wiregrass Highland families were routinely <b>Primitive Baptist</b> within a generation of arriving \u2014 the McCranies, the McInnises, the McLeans, Malcolm McMillan. Church affiliation proves nothing either way here.<br><br><b>The best untested lead:</b> Charles and Margaret McKinnon of Telfair County. Margaret was born in North Carolina about 1775; their children bracket a migration to Georgia around 1803\u201306, and the 1830 census shows four sons born 1800\u201310. Circumstantial, unproven, and the estate of Charles McKinnon is the record that would test it.',
  src:[S.bk('https://raycityhistory.wordpress.com/2019/09/14/roster-of-levi-j-knights-company-1838/'),
       S.bk('https://archive.org/details/wardshistoryofco00ward'),
       S.fg('https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/167868173/james-malcolm-mckinnon')], cen:[]},

danielluke:{n:'Daniel Luke', g:'pr', b:'about 1783', d:'after 1850',
  occ:['Road commissioner; tax collector','c',
    '<b>Road commissioner for Irwin County</b> in 1825, 1829, 1835 and 1836, and <b>tax collector of the 433rd District</b> in 1829. The order of July 1825 appointing him “to mark out river road” is the earliest Luke record this search turned up anywhere in Georgia.'],
  ev:'<b>The Luke pioneer of Irwin County — and the frontier edge of the tree.</b><br><br>The earliest Luke record found anywhere in Georgia is his: <b>July term, 1825</b>, when the Irwin County Inferior Court appointed "Daniel Luke, James Stephens and Thomas Porter… to mark out river road from lower line of the county to the Dooly line." There is <i>no</i> Luke household in the 1820 Irwin County census, so the family arrived between 1820 and 1825. He was road commissioner again in 1829, 1835 and 1836, and tax collector of the 433rd District in 1829.<br><br>A 1932 county history records that <b>"Daniel Luke married Bettie Hammonds. They moved from South Carolina on Little Peedee River"</b> and lists his children: Joshua A., Mack, Jasper, James C., John, Jesse J., David, Betsey and Mary. Every testable claim in that sketch checks out against independent records — marriages, death certificates naming parents, census birthplaces — which is why it is graded probable rather than lead.<br><br><b>A real unsolved problem:</b> the 1850 census gives Daniel M. Luke as born in <i>Georgia</i> about 1783, which sits awkwardly with sons born in South Carolina in 1803–05. And the 1830 census and tax digest each show <b>two</b> Daniel Lukes in Irwin County. Whether "our" John Luke (b. about 1798) is his son is <b>not established</b>, and should not be assumed.',
  src:[S.bk('https://archive.org/download/historyofirwinco00clem/historyofirwinco00clem_djvu.txt'),
       S.ga('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/gadeaths/id/41362'),
       S.pen('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/TestApps/id/120018')],
  cen:['c1850i']},

josephgraves:{n:'Joseph Graves', g:'l', b:'about 1796', bp:'North Carolina',
  ev:'<b>Lead only.</b> Given as Telitha Jane Graves’s father in a compiled family genealogy; married Rutheo Ogletree, also North Carolina–born. The family first appears in Georgia in the 1850 census of <b>Laurens County</b> — which is exactly where and when John Luke married Telitha, in August 1850. That coincidence is the strongest thing supporting the identification.',
  src:[S.bk('https://www.graves-fa.org/gen-histories/gens/gen382.html')], cen:[]},

benjamingraves:{n:'Benjamin Graves', g:'l', b:'before 1774', bp:'<b>North Carolina</b>',
  ev:'<b>Lead only — and the furthest back this tree currently reaches, at nine generations.</b><br><br>Recorded in <b>Brunswick County, North Carolina in the 1790 census</b>, and possibly a taxpayer in Northampton County, North Carolina in 1780, in Brunswick by 1784.<br><br>If the identification holds, he is the only ancestor in this tree documented on American soil before the Revolution ended — and the Graves line is the one that carries you out of Georgia and into colonial North Carolina.',
  src:[S.bk('https://www.graves-fa.org/gen-histories/gens/gen382.html')], cen:[]},

antneyyawn:{n:'Antney (Anthony) Yawn', g:'pr', b:'about 1815',
  ev:'<b>Probable father of Jane Yawn \u2014 and the case is now a household rather than a name.</b><br><br>The chain runs: Thomas Yawn, who died in Cook County in 1925 aged 80, has a death certificate naming his father as <b>\u201cAntney Yawn\u201d</b> and his mother as <b>\u201cSmith.\u201d</b> Elizabeth Luke n\u00e9e Yawn, who died in Clinch County in 1924 aged 69, born December 1855, has a certificate naming her father as <b>\u201cYawn\u201d</b> and her mother as <b>\u201cSmith.\u201d</b> And the 1860 Coffee County census puts that Elizabeth, aged 5, in the same boarded household as <b>Jane, aged 4.</b><br><br>So Jane and Elizabeth were sisters, and Elizabeth\u2019s father was Antney Yawn.<br><br><b>What is still missing:</b> no record names Jane\u2019s father directly. And <b>there is no Antney Yawn in Coffee County in 1860</b> \u2014 the only adult Yawn males there are Shadrach, 96, and Irwin, 25 and unmarried. The father was elsewhere, and his children were placed with neighbours. Finding him as a widower in the 1860 census of Clinch, Ware, Appling or Telfair, with a son Thomas born about 1845, would close the circle.<br><br><b>The other Yawn man in that county has now been accounted for, and it is a hard ending.</b> This page names <b>Irwin Yawn, 25 and unmarried</b>, as the only other adult Yawn male in Coffee County in 1860. He married a <b>Sarah</b>, and on <b>1 September 1862</b> he enlisted in <b>Company C, 50th Georgia Volunteers</b>.<br><br><b>He never came back, and nobody ever learned what happened to him.</b> In 1891 three Coffee County men — <b>James E. Stephens</b>, <b>Elijah Paulk</b> and <b>H. L. Paulk</b> — swore before the Ordinary that Sarah Yawn was his widow, and set down how he was lost in the plainest words the form allowed: <i>“Taken sick with measles with his command and sent to Richmond Va Hospital and has not been heard from since.”</i><br><br><b>She was still proving it twenty-nine years later.</b> Georgia pensioned widows whose husbands <i>“went to the army and have never been heard from since the war”</i> — a category the state had to print on the form because enough men had vanished to need one. <b>He is not Jane\u2019s father</b>, being unmarried in 1860 while her sisters were already born; but he is the other half of the Coffee County Yawns, and he is the reason to stop looking for him after 1862.<br><br><b>Thomas’s certificate says where he was born, and it points north.</b> Not Coffee, not Berrien, but <b>Laurens County</b> — a hundred miles up the Oconee, and the old centre of the Yawn surname in Georgia. <b>Which is a coincidence this tree has met before.</b> John Luke married Telitha Jane Graves in <b>Laurens County on 11 August 1850</b>, and the Graves family first appears in Georgia in the 1850 census of that same county. Lukes, Graveses and Yawns were all in Laurens in the same decade, and all three ended up in the same corner of the wiregrass. The <b>1850 Laurens County census</b> is now the most valuable page left unread in this file.<br><br><b>An arithmetic problem that has to be said out loud.</b> If the mother who died in childbirth in February 1860 was <b>29</b>, she was born about <b>1831</b> — which makes her <b>fourteen</b> when Thomas was born in 1845. That does not work. Either his stated age of 80 was a graveside guess, which is ordinary enough, or Antney married twice and Thomas belongs to an earlier wife. Until one of those is settled, <b>Antney stays probable and is not promoted</b>, however neatly the surnames line up.<br><br><b>The one lead left.</b> The Georgia Archives holds a <b>106-page Yawn family compilation</b> — <i>Yawn family, South Carolina &amp; Georgia</i>, compiled for Robert H. Jeffries, 1981 — and a genealogical folder on the family. Neither is digitised; both sit in the building at Morrow. That is where this gets answered, and it will take a person in a reading room rather than another search.<br><br><b>One of those two Coffee County Yawns can now be followed to the end.</b> The “Irwin, 25” of the 1860 census is almost certainly the <b>Irvin Yawn</b> whose widow Sarah applied for a pension from Willacoochee in 1891. Her affidavit says he enlisted about 1 September 1862 in <b>Company C, 55th Georgia</b>, went to the army in Virginia, and then, in her words: <i>“He was taken sick with Measels and sent to the Hospital at Richmond and has never Been heard from since the War.”</i> She signed with her mark and was still waiting twenty-nine years later. He is not Jane’s father — he was unmarried at 25 while she was already four — but he is the other half of the Yawn presence in that county, and his end is now on the record.<br><br><b>Where the marriage is not.</b> Three registers have been read through for R. B. Luke and Jane Yawn, and none of them holds it. The <b>Berrien County index, 1860—1875</b> lists seven Luke marriages and <i>no Yawn of any kind</i> — the whole letter Y runs to four names. The <b>complete Irwin County register</b>, 6,971 marriages spanning a century, carries 43 Lukes and <b>not one Yawn</b>. And <b>Coffee County Marriage Book B-1</b>, catalogued as 1871—1881, in fact opens in <b>December 1872</b> — after the marriage, and after Lucius was already born; the county’s earlier books are not in the filmed series at all. Three counties eliminated is worth nearly as much as a find, because it says where not to look again.<br><br><b>One of those two Coffee County Yawns can now be followed to the end.</b> The \u201cIrwin, 25\u201d of the 1860 census is almost certainly the <b>Irvin Yawn</b> whose widow Sarah applied for a pension from Willacoochee in 1891. Her affidavit says he enlisted about 1 September 1862 in <b>Company C, 55th Georgia</b>, was in the army in Virginia, and then, in her words: <i>\u201cHe was taken sick with Measels and sent to the Hospital at Richmond and has never Been heard from since the War.\u201d</i> She signed it with her mark and was still waiting thirty years later. He is not Jane\u2019s father \u2014 he was unmarried at 25 while she was already four \u2014 but he is the other half of the Yawn presence in that county.<br><br><b>Where the marriage is not.</b> Three registers have now been read through for R. B. Luke and Jane Yawn and none of them has it. The <b>Berrien County index, 1860\u20131875</b>, has seven Luke marriages and <i>no Yawn entry of any kind</i> \u2014 the whole letter Y runs to four names, none of them hers. The <b>complete Irwin County register</b>, 6,971 marriages across a century, has 43 Lukes and <b>not one Yawn</b>. And <b>Coffee County Marriage Book B-1</b>, catalogued as 1871\u20131881, in fact opens in <b>December 1872</b> \u2014 after the marriage, and after Lucius was already born. Coffee County\u2019s earlier books are not in the Archives\u2019 filmed series at all. That is three counties eliminated, which is worth as much as a find.',
  src:[S.ga('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/gadeaths/id/223126'),
       S.ga('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/gadeaths/id/208852'),
       S.pen('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/TestApps/id/429809'),
       S.mar('https://vault.georgiaarchives.org/digital/collection/countyfilm/id/187444')], cen:[]},

yawnmother:{n:'Elizabeth Yawn', g:'c', b:'about 1831', bp:'Georgia', d:'February 1860', dp:'Coffee County, Georgia',
  ev:'<b>She died in childbirth, and the record says so in two words.</b><br><br>The 1860 Mortality Schedule for Coffee County, Georgia \u2014 which recorded deaths in the year to 1 June 1860 \u2014 carries the entry: <b>\u201cElizabeth Yawn, 29, Female, Married, born Georgia, died February [1860], occupation Domestic, cause of death: Child Bed.\u201d</b><br><br>Four months later the census enumerator found her three surviving daughters \u2014 Elizabeth 5, Jane 4, Polly Ann 1 \u2014 boarded in a neighbour\u2019s house with no parent present. <b>Polly Ann, aged one, was almost certainly the baby she died having.</b><br><br>Her own surname was probably <b>Smith</b>: two of her children\u2019s death certificates, filed sixty-five years later in two different counties, each name the mother simply as \u201cSmith.\u201d<br><br>She is the reason this branch of the family scattered, and she is four lines in a mortality schedule.',
  src:[S.bk('http://genealogytrails.com/geo/coffee/1860-mortality.html'),
       S.cen('https://iiif.archive.org/iiif/populationschedu117unit%24530/full/full/0/default.jpg')], cen:[]},

josephricketson:{n:'Joseph Ricketson', g:'l', b:'1784', bp:'Burke County, Georgia', d:'1820', dp:'Montgomery County, Georgia',
  ev:'<b>The missing generation now has a name and a date.</b> This tree has said for a while that John P. Ricketson is <i>probably</i> a grandson of Joseph through “one of his adult sons” — an abstraction standing in for a man nobody could name. Folks Huxford’s <i>History of Clinch County</i> (1916) names him. In the list of Justices of the Peace it records: <b>Joseph Ricketson, commissioned 14 January 1841, term 1841—1845</b>, for the <b>586th or Mud Creek District</b>.<br><br><b>That is the same district.</b> Serena Ricketson, widowed, farmed in the 586th; James M. McKinnon was enumerated in the 586th in 1840. A Joseph Ricketson holding the county office of Justice of the Peace there in 1841 cannot be Joseph who died in 1820 — so he is an adult Ricketson of exactly the missing generation, in exactly the right place, at exactly the right time.<br><br>It still does not prove John P. is his son. But the hypothesis has stopped being a shape and become a person, and he is worth looking for in the Ware and Clinch records.<br><br><b>Lead only \u2014 and the caveat matters more than the entry.</b> Huxford sketches Joseph Ricketson of Montgomery County, born in Burke County in 1784, married <b>Serena Foy Arnold</b> (widow of Needham Arnold, daughter of George Foy, born North Carolina) on 17 July 1806 in Tattnall County. Nine children, born 1807\u20131821.<br><br><b>John P. Ricketson\u2019s link into this family is NOT proven.</b> He was born in 1828, eight years after Joseph died, so he cannot be a son. He is most plausibly a grandson through one of Joseph\u2019s adult sons \u2014 Allen (b. 1807), Joseph (b. 1809), Benjamin Franklin (b. 1810) or George (b. 1812) \u2014 all grown men in the right place at the right time. That is a hypothesis, not a finding, and everything above this point in the Ricketson line hangs on it.<br><br><b>One detail that is worth the whole entry:</b> after Joseph died, his widow Serena moved to Ware County and farmed in the <b>586th Militia District</b> \u2014 the exact district where \u201cKINON, James M.\u201d was enumerated in 1840. <b>The Ricketsons and the McKinnons were neighbours three generations before Monroe married Mary Jane.</b>',
  src:[S.bk('https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/ssd?id=uga1.32108009929509;seq=267')], cen:[]},

serenafoy:{n:'Serena Foy', g:'l', b:'about 1785', bp:'North Carolina', d:'1856', dp:'Coffee County, Georgia',
  ev:'<b>Lead only</b>, through the unproven link above. Daughter of George Foy; married first Needham Arnold, then Joseph Ricketson on 17 July 1806 in Tattnall County.<br><br>Widowed in 1820 with nine children, she moved to Ware County and bought a farm in the 586th Militia District. That land was cut out of Ware into Clinch in 1850, and out of Clinch into Coffee in 1854 \u2014 <b>she died in Coffee County in 1856 without ever moving again.</b> Three counties, one farm. It is the neatest illustration in this tree of why these families read as Ware, Clinch, Coffee and Atkinson in different records.<br><br>Her census references run 1830, 1840 and 1850, Ware, as a widow.',
  src:[S.bk('https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/ssd?id=uga1.32108009929509;seq=267')], cen:[]},

timothysr:{n:'Timothy Ricketson Sr.', g:'l', b:'before 1760', d:'after 1784',
  occ:['Soldier, Continental service','l',
    'Granted bounty land in 1784 for Revolutionary service. The grant is a real record; the line of descent from him down to this family is not.'],
  ev:'<b>Lead only \u2014 and the deepest name currently attached to this tree, at ten generations.</b><br><br>Huxford records him as a <b>Revolutionary soldier</b>, granted <b>287\u00bd acres of bounty land in Washington County, Georgia, on 20 November 1784</b> \u2014 land given for service in the war. Georgia headright plats independently show Ricketsons taking grants in Richmond, Washington and Franklin Counties in 1784\u201385, under plainly English given names: Timothy, Jordan, Marmaduke.<br><br><b>Everything here depends on an unproven link</b> \u2014 John P. Ricketson\u2019s connection to Joseph Ricketson\u2019s family. Treat this as the direction the line probably runs, not as an ancestor established. If it holds, he is the only man in this tree documented fighting in the Revolution.',
  src:[S.bk('https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/ssd?id=uga1.32108009929509;seq=267')], cen:[]},

leonardharper:{n:'Leonard Harper', g:'pr', b:'before 1800', d:'before 1845',
  ev:'<b>The first Harper in Irwin County.</b> He appears in the 1830 census and in the 1830 tax digest — the earliest that survives — and is absent from the 1820 census, so the Harpers arrived in the same window as the Lukes. His will was <b>proven at the January 1843 term</b> of the Irwin County court and recorded on 26 January 1843 — this site previously said 1845, from a secondary sketch, and the record book corrects it.<br><br>A 1932 county history says he <b>"came from McIntosh County, Georgia"</b> and reared five sons and three daughters. That is a notably different origin story from the Lukes’ — it implies the Harpers entered Georgia by the <b>coast</b>, not overland from the Carolinas.<br><br><b>And here the family record turns uncomfortable, and now it names people.</b> <b>His will, dictated 29 June 1842, held three human beings and divided them among his children.</b><br><br><i>“… I give and bequeath unto my beloved wife <b>Susannah Harper</b> all my real and personal estate together with all my notes debts dues and credits during her natural life … <b>first</b> I do give and bequeath to my son <b>John L. B. Harper</b> and <b>Lewis L. C. Harper</b> a negro woman named <b>Rose</b>; <b>Second</b> I give and bequeath unto my son <b>Henry S. C. Harper</b> and <b>Fleming B. Harper</b> a negro boy named <b>Tom</b>; <b>Third</b> I give and bequeath unto my son <b>George J. M. Harper</b> and <b>Hester E. M. Fulton</b> a negro boy named ——”</i><br><br><b>Rose and Tom are read off the manuscript and are not in doubt.</b> The third boy’s name is three or four letters in a hurried hand — most likely <i>Joe</i>, and left blank here rather than guessed at.<br><br><b>The will also settles Susannah.</b> She was carried on this site as <i>probably</i> his widow; he names her as his wife. It explains the 1860 schedule exactly — his whole estate came to her for her natural life, and eighteen years later Susan Harper of Irwin County, aged 75, born South Carolina, was still holding <b>two men, aged 33 and 30</b>. If Tom was a boy of eight or fifteen in 1842 he was a man of twenty-six to thirty-three in 1860, and may be one of them.',
  src:[S.bk('https://archive.org/download/historyofirwinco00clem/historyofirwinco00clem_djvu.txt'),
       S.sl('http://us-census.org/pub/usgenweb/census/ga/irwin/1860/slave.txt')], cen:[]}
};

